= Viscous fluid flow
{wiki}

Viscous fluid flow includes shear stresses proportional to velocity gradients for a Newtonian fluid.

= Euler limit
{c}
{parent=Viscous fluid flow}

= Inviscid limit
{synonym}

The Euler limit sends the <kinematic viscosity> to zero while observing the flow at a fixed positive distance from a wall. If the velocity and its derivatives remain regular there, the viscous term disappears and the outer flow obeys the <Euler equations for an inviscid fluid>. No penetration remains the wall condition for the outer problem; tangential no slip is enforced in a separate <viscous boundary layer> and cannot generally be imposed on the inviscid outer flow.

= Inviscid core
{parent=Euler limit}

An inviscid core is the part of a high-<Reynolds number> flow outside thin wall or shear layers, where <viscosity> is negligible at the retained asymptotic order. It obeys the <Euler equations for an inviscid fluid>. Its tangential velocity need not satisfy no slip at a material wall; adjoining <viscous boundary layers> supply that condition and can influence the core through displacement and pressure matching.

= Pressure-driven channel flow
{parent=Viscous fluid flow}
{title2=$\mu\Delta_\perp w=-G$}

A steady unidirectional incompressible viscous flow in a rigid channel, driven by $G=-dp/dz$, reduces the axial <momentum> equation to the displayed <Poisson equation>, with $w=0$ on the cross-sectional boundary. Circular-pipe flow is the <Hagen-Poiseuille flow> special case; an arbitrary cross section need not have that parabolic radial profile.

= Corner expansion of pressure-driven sector flow
{parent=Pressure-driven channel flow}
{title2=$\lambda_n=(2n+1)\pi/(2\alpha)$}

In a symmetric wedge $|\theta|<\alpha$, a constant Poisson forcing admits the particular solution $r^2(\cos2\theta/\cos2\alpha-1)/4$, except at resonant angles. Zero-wall harmonic corrections have exponents $\lambda_n=(2n+1)\pi/(2\alpha)$. If the smallest exponent is below two, the global boundary data make that homogeneous mode dominate the local quadratic forcing. If it is below one, the <velocity> gradient can be singular although the <velocity> vanishes. When an exponent equals two, the appropriate radial solution contains $r^2\log r$, since $\Delta(r^2\log r\cos2\theta)=4\cos2\theta$.

= Plane Poiseuille flow
{parent=Viscous fluid flow}
{wiki}

= Symmetric viscous interaction in an indented Poiseuille channel
{parent=Plane Poiseuille flow}

At large <Reynolds number> $\mathcal R$, a long indentation of relative height $\epsilon$ has nonlinear near-wall interaction when the streamwise scale ratio satisfies $\lambda/\mathcal R=\epsilon^3$. The regime $\mathcal R^{1/7}\ll\lambda\ll\mathcal R$ gives a thin viscous layer and an effectively common <pressure> across the core. The core displacement is $u_1=A(x)U'_0(y)$ and $v_1=-A'(x)U_0(y)$. A lower wall displaced to $\epsilon F(x)$ produces layer displacement $F+A$, while the upper wall produces $-A$. Uniqueness for their common <pressure> forcing and upstream data makes the two layer displacements equal, so $A=-F/2$.

= One-third-power similarity of an indented channel boundary layer
{parent=Symmetric viscous interaction in an indented Poiseuille channel}
{title2=$G'''+\frac23GG''-\frac13(G')^2=\frac23\bar p$}

For indentation $F=bx^{1/3}$, the common layer displacement is $H=bx^{1/3}/2$. The <boundary layer> <stream function> $\psi=x^{2/3}G(zx^{-1/3})$ gives <velocity> $U=x^{1/3}G'$ and <pressure> $P=\bar p x^{2/3}$. Substitution into the <Prandtl boundary-layer equation> yields the displayed equation, with $G(0)=G'(0)=0$ and $G'\sim6\eta+3b$. This is a conditional similarity construction: the constant $\bar p$ must permit a solution of the <boundary value problem>.

= Two-layer plane Poiseuille flow with unequal viscosities
{parent=Plane Poiseuille flow}
{title2=$u_\pm=U_0+Cy/\mu_\pm-Gy^2/(2\mu_\pm)$}

Two viscous layers occupy $-a<y<0$ and $0<y<a$, with viscosities $\mu_-$ and $\mu_+$. A uniform driving <pressure gradient> $G=-p_x>0$, a stationary flat interface and no-slip outer walls give the displayed <plane Poiseuille flow>, where $U_0=Ga^2/(\mu_-+\mu_+)$ and $C=Ga(\mu_--\mu_+)/[2(\mu_-+\mu_+)]$. These constants follow from <continuity> of velocity and shear stress at the interface and zero wall velocities. The upper-to-lower volume-flux ratio is $(1+7r)/[r(7+r)]$, with $r=\mu_+/\mu_-$. As $r\to\infty$, the interface becomes effectively stationary, the upper flux vanishes, and the lower layer approaches flow between two stationary plates a distance $a$ apart. The upper wall stress remains finite despite its vanishing velocity gradient.

= Fully developed flow
{parent=Viscous fluid flow}
{title2=$\mathbf u=u(z)\hat{\mathbf x}$}

A fully developed parallel flow has a velocity profile independent of the streamwise coordinate. In a planar channel a representative profile is $\mathbf u=u(z)\hat{\mathbf x}$; wall conditions and translational symmetry remove cross-stream motion. The streamwise <pressure gradient> may remain nonzero, while the convective acceleration vanishes. Fully developed flow is a modeling branch, not a uniqueness assertion for all flows in that geometry.

= Velocity profile
{parent=Viscous fluid flow}

A velocity profile records the variation of <velocity> across a spatial coordinate, for example $u(z)$ across a parallel viscous layer. Its derivative sets the tangential <shear stress> in a <Newtonian fluid>, while its integral gives the <volume flux>.

= Viscous boundary layer
{parent=Viscous fluid flow}

A viscous boundary layer is a <boundary layer> next to a surface in which viscosity remains important even when the outer flow has a large <Reynolds number>. It reconciles the outer inviscid velocity with the <no-slip boundary condition>.

= Prandtl limit
{c}
{parent=Viscous boundary layer}
{title2=$\delta/x\sim\mathrm{Re}_x^{-1/2}$}

The Prandtl limit observes a high-<Reynolds number> flow within a shrinking wall-normal layer. For streamwise distance $x$ and outer speed $U$, rescale the normal distance by $\delta=\sqrt{\nu x/U}$ and the normal velocity by $U\delta/x$. Convection and normal viscous diffusion then remain comparable, while streamwise viscous diffusion is smaller. The leading normal momentum equation makes pressure independent of the rescaled normal coordinate, and the tangential equation is the <Prandtl boundary-layer equation>. This laminar asymptotic balance is local and need not remain valid through separation or instability.

= Prandtl boundary-layer equation
{c}
{parent=Viscous boundary layer}

For a steady two-dimensional incompressible boundary layer with tangential velocity $u(x,y)$, normal velocity $v(x,y)$, outer tangential velocity $U(x)$, and <kinematic viscosity> $\nu$,
$$
u u_x+v u_y=U\frac{dU}{dx}+\nu u_{yy},
\qquad
u_x+v_y=0.
$$
The pressure is uniform across the layer, and the outer Euler equation supplies $-p_x/\rho=U\,dU/dx$.

= Blasius equation
{c}
{parent=Prandtl boundary-layer equation}
{title2=$f'''+\tfrac12ff''=0$}

For a steady incompressible <viscous boundary layer> over a flat plate with constant outer speed $U$, the <stream function> $\psi=\sqrt{\nu Ux}\,f(y\sqrt{U/(\nu x)})$ reduces the <Prandtl boundary-layer equation> to the displayed ordinary differential equation. The wall and outer conditions are $f(0)=f'(0)=0$ and $f'(\infty)=1$. The wall shear is $\rho U\sqrt{\nu U/x}\,f''(0)$, so its integral on one plate face of length $L$ is $2\rho U^2Lf''(0)/\sqrt{UL/\nu}$. Both equally exposed faces contribute twice this value.

= Unsteady Prandtl equation
{parent=Prandtl boundary-layer equation}
{title2=$u_t+uu_x+vu_y=\mathcal U_t+\mathcal U\mathcal U_x+\nu u_{yy},\quad u_x+v_y=0$}

The unsteady form of the <Prandtl boundary-layer equation> evolves the tangential boundary-layer <velocity> under advection, outer pressure forcing and normal viscous diffusion. Incompressibility supplies the normal <velocity>. A stationary wall imposes the <no-slip boundary condition>, and tangential <velocity> matches the prescribed outer slip <velocity> far from the wall. The normal far-field disturbance need not vanish.

= Linearized unsteady Prandtl equation
{parent=Unsteady Prandtl equation}
{title2=$\tilde u_t+U\tilde u_x+V\tilde u_y+U_x\tilde u+U_y\tilde v=\nu\tilde u_{yy}$}

For a fixed outer pressure and slip <velocity>, retain terms linear in a disturbance of a base flow $(U,V)$. The tangential equation has the displayed form and continuity is $\tilde u_x+\tilde v_y=0$. Disturbances vanish at the wall and the tangential disturbance vanishes in the far field. A local parallel-profile approximation may freeze the coefficients even when the profile is not an exact global steady solution.

= Prandtl normal-mode equation
{c}
{parent=Linearized unsteady Prandtl equation}
{title2=$\nu v'''=i\alpha[(U-c)v'-U'v]$}

For a frozen real parallel profile and modes proportional to $e^{i\alpha(x-ct)}$, continuity eliminates tangential <velocity> and gives this third-order equation. Wall conditions are $v=v'=0$; outer tangential matching gives $v'\to0$, with bounded $v$. Real coefficients give the spectral symmetry $(\alpha,c)\mapsto(-\alpha,c^*)$. For positive real <wavenumber>, growth means $\operatorname{Im}c>0$.

= Boundary-layer thickness from an advection-diffusion balance
{parent=Viscous boundary layer}

For streamwise length $L$, outer speed $U$, and layer thickness $\delta$, balancing $u u_x\sim U^2/L$ with $\nu u_{yy}\sim\nu U/\delta^2$ gives
$$
\frac\delta L\sim\operatorname{Re}^{-1/2},
\qquad
\operatorname{Re}=\frac{UL}{\nu}.
$$

= Viscous diffusion time
{title2=$L^2/\nu$}
{parent=Viscous fluid flow}

Momentum diffuses across a distance $L$ in the characteristic time $L^2/\nu$, where $\nu$ is the <kinematic viscosity>.

= Couette flow
{parent=Viscous fluid flow}
{c}
{wiki}

Couette flow is viscous shear flow driven by relative tangential motion of parallel boundaries. In the steady no-pressure-gradient case its velocity profile is linear.

= Couette-Poiseuille flow
{parent=Couette flow}
{c}
{title2=$u(y)=Uy/a+(G/2)y(a-y)$}

<Couette-Poiseuille flow> superposes wall-driven shear and pressure-driven parabolic flow between no-slip parallel plates. With one plate moving at $U$, separation $a$, and $dp/dx=-\rho\nu G$, the displayed profile has volume flux $Ua/2+Ga^3/12$ per unit width. Requiring zero flux gives $G=-6U/a^2$ and $u=U[3(y/a)^2-2y/a]$, with a pressure gradient induced by return-flow constraints.

= Zero-flux Couette-Poiseuille flow
{parent=Couette flow}
{title2=$u/U=3(y/h)^2-2y/h$}

Between a resting wall at $y=0$ and a wall moving at speed $U$ at $y=h$, an adverse <pressure gradient> $p_x=G$ gives
$$
u(y)=-\frac G{2\mu}y(h-y)+\frac{Uy}{h}.
$$
Zero <volume flux> requires $G=6\mu U/h^2$, yielding $u/U=3(y/h)^2-2y/h$. Reverse flow below $2h/3$ balances the positive flux near the moving wall. The minimum is $-U/3$ at $h/3$.

= Two-layer Couette flow
{parent=Couette flow}

In two-layer Couette flow, each Newtonian-fluid layer has a linear velocity profile. Continuity of velocity and <shear stress> at the interface makes the velocity drop across each layer proportional to its thickness divided by its <dynamic viscosity>.

= Falling film flow
{parent=Viscous fluid flow}
{wiki}

A steady film down a wall has a parabolic velocity profile set by gravity, ambient pressure gradient, and surface shear.

= Two-layer falling film of equal-density viscous fluids
{parent=Falling film flow}

Let the lower fluid have thickness $h$ and <viscosity> $\mu$, and the upper fluid thickness $\alpha h$ and <viscosity> $\beta\mu$, with common density $\rho$. For steady flow down a plane inclined at angle $\theta$, set $H=(1+\alpha)h$, $A=\rho g\sin\theta/\mu$. The common <hydrostatic pressure> is $p_{\rm atm}+\rho g\cos\theta(H-y)$. No slip at the wall, continuous <velocity> and <shear stress> at $y=h$, and zero upper surface shear give $u_1=A(Hy-y^2/2)$ and $u_2(h+z)=Ah^2(\alpha+1/2)+(A/\beta)(\alpha hz-z^2/2)$. These follow by integrating $\mu_i u_i''=-\rho g\sin\theta$ in each layer. The stress applied to the lower fluid equals the downslope weight of the upper layer, independent of its <viscosity>; the upper <viscosity> affects its shear rate but not that transmitted stress.

= Residual wall films in a receding gravity current
{parent=Falling film flow}

A falling <free surface> does not instantaneously dry previously wetted rigid walls. Thin films remain above the bulk liquid and drain under the component of <gravitational acceleration> parallel to the walls. For a film of thickness $b$ over a wetted distance $\ell$, <falling film flow> gives a speed of order $gb^2/\nu$, hence $b\sim(\nu\ell/(gt))^{1/2}$ at late times for an order-one wall angle. These films can be visible in a cross-section while holding asymptotically negligible volume. In a <V-shaped channel gravity current>, local bulk depth falls only as $t^{-1/7}$; at a fixed nonzero station with a finite previous maximum wetted height, film volume decays as $t^{-1/2}$ while bulk-sectional volume decays as $t^{-2/7}$. Thus the film does not change the leading spreading law.

= Inclined viscous film with opposing surface shear
{parent=Falling film flow}

For a film of thickness $h$ flowing down a slope of angle $\alpha$, an upslope surface stress of magnitude $\tau$ gives
$$
u(z)=\frac{\rho g\sin\alpha}{\mu}
\left(hz-\frac{z^2}{2}\right)-\frac{\tau z}{\mu}.
$$
The surface velocity, total flux, and basal shear reverse at
$$
\tau=\frac12\rho gh\sin\alpha,
\qquad
\frac23\rho gh\sin\alpha,
\qquad
\rho gh\sin\alpha,
$$
respectively.

= Stress boundary condition
{parent=Viscous fluid flow}
{wiki}

At a fluid interface, normal and tangential tractions satisfy the imposed stress balance.

= Stress-free boundary condition
{parent=Stress boundary condition}

A clean fluid interface with negligible exterior viscosity has zero tangential traction. Its normal traction is determined by exterior pressure and surface tension.

= No-penetration boundary condition
{parent=Stress boundary condition}

= Impermeability condition
{synonym}

The no-penetration boundary condition sets the relative normal velocity of a fluid and an impermeable boundary to zero. Tangential slip may still be permitted.

= No-slip boundary condition
{parent=Viscous fluid flow}
{wiki}

No slip requires fluid velocity to equal solid-boundary velocity.

= Pressure compatibility at a no-slip wall
{parent=No-slip boundary condition}

For a smooth incompressible <Navier-Stokes equation> trajectory with zero <velocity> at a fixed flat wall, the normal momentum equation determines the normal <pressure gradient>. In a perturbation model with buoyancy term $\mathrm{Ri}_B\theta\hat{\mathbf y}$, it gives $\partial_np=\nu\mathbf n\cdot\Delta\mathbf u-\mathrm{Ri}_B\theta\,\mathbf n\cdot\hat{\mathbf y}$. An extra homogeneous pressure <Neumann boundary condition> is valid only when the right side vanishes; it does not follow from the <no-slip boundary condition>.

= Taylor-expanded no-slip boundary condition
{c}
{parent=No-slip boundary condition}

For a displaced wall $y=\epsilon h_1(x,t)$, write fluid velocity as $\mathbf u=\epsilon\mathbf u_1+\epsilon^2\mathbf u_2+\cdots$ and prescribed wall velocity as $\epsilon\mathbf v_1+\epsilon^2\mathbf v_2+\cdots$. A <Taylor expansion> about $y=0$ gives $\mathbf u_1(0)=\mathbf v_1$ and $\mathbf u_2(0)=\mathbf v_2-h_1\partial_y\mathbf u_1(0)$. The second-order correction converts first-order velocity gradients into a mean boundary flow.

= Newtonian fluid
{c}
{parent=Viscous fluid flow}
{wiki}

A Newtonian fluid has viscous <stress> proportional to the local <rate-of-strain tensor>, with coefficients independent of that rate. For an isotropic fluid these coefficients are viscosities. Its <Newtonian fluid stress tensor> specifies the corresponding constitutive relation; the material and its <stress> <tensor> are distinct concepts.

= Newtonian fluid stress tensor
{c}
{parent=Newtonian fluid}
{title2=$\boldsymbol\sigma=-pI+\mu[\nabla\mathbf u+(\nabla\mathbf u)^T]$}

For isotropic <incompressible flow> of a <Newtonian fluid>, the <stress> <tensor> is the displayed expression, with <pressure> $p$, <dynamic viscosity> $\mu$, <velocity> $\mathbf u$, and <identity matrix> $I$. In <Couette flow>, its off-diagonal component is the <shear stress>.

= Shear stress
{title2=$\tau$}
{parent=Newtonian fluid stress tensor}
{wiki}

Shear stress is the tangential component of traction on a surface. For leading unidirectional flow $u(y)$ in a Newtonian fluid, $\tau=\mu\,du/dy$.

= Shear velocity
{parent=Shear stress}
{title2=$u_*$}

= Friction velocity
{synonym}

The <shear velocity> is the <velocity> scale $u_*=\sqrt{|\tau|/\rho}$ associated with a <shear stress> $\tau$ in a fluid of <mass density> $\rho$. It is a stress scale, not automatically the actual local fluid <velocity> at a grain.

= Strain-rate tensor
{parent=Newtonian fluid stress tensor}
{wiki}

= Rate-of-strain tensor
{synonym}

The rate-of-strain tensor is the symmetric part of the velocity gradient,
$$
e=\frac12\left(\nabla u+(\nabla u)^T\right).
$$

= Principal strain rates
{parent=Strain-rate tensor}
{title2=$a,b,c$}

<Principal strain rates> are the <eigenvalues> $a,b,c$ of the symmetric <rate-of-strain tensor>. Positive values are local extension and negative values local compression. In <incompressible flow> $a+b+c=0$, implying $a^3+b^3+c^3=3abc$. Their <eigenvectors> describe the principal deformation directions.

= Bi-axial strain
{parent=Principal strain rates}

Bi-axial extension has two positive <principal strain rates> and one negative rate in <incompressible flow>. It flattens a material volume toward a sheet while extending its two in-plane directions. A negative mean product of the three rates indicates amplitude-weighted preference for this geometry, but alone does not establish an unweighted event-frequency majority.

= Shear rate
{title2=$\dot\gamma$}
{parent=Strain-rate tensor}
{wiki}

The shear rate is the scalar rate at which adjacent material layers acquire shear strain. In simple shear $u_x=u_x(y)$, its signed value is $\dot\gamma=du_x/dy$.

= Simple shear flow
{parent=Shear rate}
{wiki}

A simple shear flow has velocity $\mathbf u=(\dot\gamma y,0,0)$ in Cartesian coordinates. Its velocity gradient has one nonzero off-diagonal entry.

= Spin tensor
{title2=$\boldsymbol\Omega$}
{parent=Strain-rate tensor}
{wiki}

The spin tensor is the antisymmetric part of the velocity gradient, $\boldsymbol\Omega=(\nabla\mathbf u-(\nabla\mathbf u)^T)/2$. It describes local rigid-body rotation.

= Polar shear stress in a Newtonian fluid
{parent=Newtonian fluid stress tensor}

For a planar velocity field in polar coordinates,
$$
\sigma_{r\theta}
=\mu\left[
r\frac{\partial}{\partial r}\left(\frac{u_\theta}{r}\right)
+\frac1r\frac{\partial u_r}{\partial\theta}
\right].
$$

= Navier-Stokes equation
{parent=Viscous fluid flow}
{c}
{wiki=Navier–Stokes_equations}

For an incompressible Newtonian fluid of constant density and viscosity,
$$
\rho\left(\partial_tu+u\mathbin{\cdot}\nabla u\right)
=-\nabla p+\mu\nabla^2u+\rho f,
\qquad \nabla\mathbin{\cdot}u=0.
$$

= Pressure Poisson equation for incompressible flow
{parent=Navier-Stokes equation}
{title2=$\nabla^2(p/\rho)=-\partial_i\partial_j(u_iu_j)$}

Taking the divergence of the constant-density <Navier-Stokes equation> and using <incompressibility> gives the displayed elliptic <Poisson equation> for pressure. Its <Green function> is nonlocal. For an isolated eddy, the far pressure has a quadrupolar contribution proportional to $r^{-3}$, obtained by differentiating the kernel $1/r$ twice. Boundary conditions can contribute additional pressure fields.

= Long-range pressure correlations in turbulence
{parent=Pressure Poisson equation for incompressible flow}
{title2=$\langle u_x^2p'\rangle=O(r^{-3})$}

The quadrupolar kernel of the <pressure Poisson equation for incompressible flow> allows mixed pressure-velocity correlations with an $r^{-3}$ tail. Its derivative in the evolution of a longitudinal triple correlation allows an $r^{-4}$ tail, potentially changing the <Loitsyansky integral>. The existence of a nonlocal kernel does not fix the tail coefficient or prevent cancellations. An isotropic mean pressure multipole can vanish while a mixed higher-order correlation remains nonzero.

= Leray-Helmholtz projection
{c}
{parent=Navier-Stokes equation}
{title2=$P_\sigma$}

The Leray-Helmholtz projection $P_\sigma$ is the orthogonal projection of square-integrable vector fields onto divergence-free vector fields. Applying it to an incompressible momentum equation removes the pressure gradient.

= Stokes operator
{c}
{parent=Navier-Stokes equation}
{wiki}

The Stokes operator is $A=-P_\sigma\Delta$ on divergence-free vector fields, where $P_\sigma$ is the <Leray-Helmholtz projection>. On a periodic domain it equals $-\Delta$ on its domain, and its inverse gains two <Sobolev space>[Sobolev derivatives].

= Spectral projection of the Stokes operator
{title2=$P_N$}
{parent=Stokes operator}

The spectral projection $P_N$ retains the first $N$ Stokes eigenfunctions. On its finite-dimensional range, one derivative costs at most $\lambda_N^{1/2}$ and all Sobolev norms are equivalent.

= Skew-symmetry of incompressible transport
{parent=Navier-Stokes equation}

For a periodic or boundary-tangent <divergence-free vector field> $u$, <integration by parts> gives
$$
\int (u\mathbin\cdot\nabla f)g
=-\int (u\mathbin\cdot\nabla g)f.
$$
Consequently $\int(u\mathbin\cdot\nabla f)f=0$, the cancellation underlying many fluid <energy estimates>.

= Skew-symmetrized transport form
{parent=Skew-symmetry of incompressible transport}

For a velocity field that need not be divergence free, the corrected transport
$$
\widetilde B(u,v)=(u\mathbin\cdot\nabla)v+\frac12(\nabla\mathbin\cdot u)v
$$
satisfies $\langle\widetilde B(u,v),w\rangle=-\langle\widetilde B(u,w),v\rangle$ and therefore preserves the quadratic energy cancellation.

= Navier-Stokes equation with spectrally truncated advection
{parent=Navier-Stokes equation}

Replacing the advecting velocity by a fixed finite Stokes projection $P_Nu$ makes the three-dimensional nonlinearity controlled by $\lambda_N^{1/4}|u|\|v\|\|w\|$. This yields global existence and uniqueness at the weak energy level.

= Steady skew-symmetrized Navier-Stokes equation
{parent=Navier-Stokes equation}

The steady equation $\nu Au+\widetilde B(u,u)=f$ retains the energy cancellation without imposing incompressibility. Galerkin bounds, compactness, and elliptic bootstrapping produce an $H^2$ periodic solution for square-integrable forcing.

= Rayleigh-Bénard convection
{c}
{parent=Navier-Stokes equation}
{wiki}

Rayleigh-Bénard convection is buoyancy-driven flow caused by heating a fluid from below. In an infinite-Prandtl-number reduction, velocity is determined instantaneously by a forced <Stokes operator>[Stokes equation], while temperature satisfies an advection-diffusion equation.

= Four-thirds convective heat-transfer law
{parent=Rayleigh-Bénard convection}
{title2=$q\propto(\Delta T)^{4/3}$}

A high-<Rayleigh number> closure $\mathrm{Nu}=C\mathrm{Ra}^{1/3}$ gives $q=Ck_T[g\beta/(\nu\kappa)]^{1/3}(\Delta T)^{4/3}$, independent of layer depth. It assumes boundary-layer-controlled <heat transfer>, a well-mixed interior, fixed material properties and a fixed <Prandtl number> regime. It is a model, not a universal asymptotic law for every turbulent convection regime or for an impulsive initial conductive transient.

= Convective cooling under the four-thirds heat-transfer law
{parent=Four-thirds convective heat-transfer law}
{title2=$\theta(t)=[\theta_0^{-1/3}+Kt/3]^{-3}$}

A well-mixed layer of depth $h$ losing heat through an unstable cold upper boundary has bulk excess temperature $\theta=T-T_0$. With <four-thirds convective heat-transfer law> flux $q=Ck[g\beta/(\nu\kappa)]^{1/3}\theta^{4/3}$, its heat capacity per area is $kh/\kappa$. Thus $\dot\theta=-K\theta^{4/3}$ with $K=C\kappa[g\beta/(\nu\kappa)]^{1/3}/h$, giving the displayed cooling law. It excludes the initial conductive transient and the late low-Rayleigh conduction regime.

= Stress-free convection growth-rate polynomial
{parent=Rayleigh-Bénard convection}
{title2=$(\sigma+\mathrm{Pr}a^2)(\sigma+a^2)=\mathrm{Ra}\,\mathrm{Pr}\lambda^2/a^2$}

For a fixed-temperature layer with <stress-free boundary conditions>, a vertical <Fourier sine series> has $a^2=\lambda^2+n^2\pi^2$, where $\lambda$ is horizontal <wavenumber>. The coupled vertical <velocity> and temperature amplitudes satisfy the displayed quadratic for their <growth rate> $\sigma$. Its roots are real for positive <Rayleigh number>, while both roots have negative real parts for negative <Rayleigh number>. Setting $\sigma=0$ gives the <free-slip convection neutral curve>.

= Exchange of stabilities in stress-free convection
{parent=Stress-free convection growth-rate polynomial}
{title2=$\sigma_c=0$}

In nonrotating <Rayleigh-Bénard convection> with fixed-temperature, <stress-free boundary conditions>, the <stress-free convection growth-rate polynomial> has real roots on the destabilizing side. Consequently the first instability is stationary, rather than oscillatory. Minimizing the <free-slip convection neutral curve> selects $\lambda_c=\pi/\sqrt2$ and $\mathrm{Ra}_c=27\pi^4/4$.

= Thermal-diffusion scaling of a convection layer
{parent=Rayleigh-Bénard convection}
{title2=$t_\kappa=d^2/\kappa,\ u_\kappa=\kappa/d$}

Using layer depth $d$ and <thermal diffusivity> $\kappa$ gives time scale $d^2/\kappa$, <velocity> scale $\kappa/d$, and perturbation <pressure> scale $\rho_0\kappa^2/d^2$. With temperature scale $\Delta T$, the <linearized Boussinesq equations> have viscous coefficient <Prandtl number> $\mathrm{Pr}=\nu/\kappa$ and buoyancy coefficient $\mathrm{Ra}\,\mathrm{Pr}$, where <Rayleigh number> $\mathrm{Ra}=g\alpha_T\Delta T d^3/(\nu\kappa)$.

= Conductive state of Rayleigh-Bénard convection
{parent=Rayleigh-Bénard convection}
{title2=$\boldsymbol u_b=0,\ T_b=T_0+\Delta T(1-z/d)$}

The conductive state is the no-flow equilibrium in which <heat conduction> alone maintains a linear <temperature> profile across a layer with fixed boundary temperatures. Its <hydrostatic pressure> follows by integrating $P_b'=-g\rho_b$, with <mass density> obtained from the <equation of state>. Under the <Boussinesq approximation>, deviations from this state are the variables used in the linear <Rayleigh-Bénard convection> problem.

= Rotating Rayleigh-Bénard convection
{parent=Rayleigh-Bénard convection}

A thermally driven fluid layer rotating about the vertical axis experiences a <Coriolis force> that couples vertical <velocity> to vertical <vorticity>. Its onset depends on the <Rayleigh number>, <Prandtl number> and <Taylor number>, as well as thermal and mechanical <boundary conditions>. The force does no mechanical work, but changes the linear mode balance and nonlinear <convection roll> competition.

= Five-mode rotating-convection truncation
{parent=Rotating Rayleigh-Bénard convection}

A <Galerkin method> retaining one <stream function> mode, two temperature modes and two transverse-velocity modes produces five coupled amplitudes. A useful normalization gives $a'=-\sigma a+\sigma rb-\sigma^2qd$, $b'=a-b-ac$, $c'=h(-c+ab)$, $d'=a-\sigma d-ae$, $e'=-(4-h)\sigma e+had$. Here $\sigma>0$ is the <Prandtl number>, $0<h<4$, and $q$ measures squared rotation. The trivial <equilibrium point> has characteristic polynomial $(s+\sigma)^2(s+1)-\sigma r(s+\sigma)+\sigma^2q(s+1)$ in its three coupled linear modes; the remaining two modes decay. <Stationary bifurcations> and oscillatory onset meet at $q=(1+\sigma)/(1-\sigma)$, $r=2/(1-\sigma)$ when $\sigma<1$.

= Counterpropagating Hopf amplitudes in rotating convection
{parent=Rotating Rayleigh-Bénard convection}
{title2=$\dot Z_\pm=rZ_\pm-(g_s|Z_\pm|^2+g_c|Z_\mp|^2)Z_\pm$}

Near an oscillatory onset of <rotating Rayleigh-Bénard convection>, opposite travelling roll waves have coupled <Hopf bifurcation> amplitudes. Symmetry gives the displayed cubic <amplitude equations>, with generally complex self-coupling and cross-coupling coefficients. Write $a_s=\operatorname{Re}g_s$ and $a_c=\operatorname{Re}g_c$. For $r>0,a_s>0$, a single travelling wave has intensity $r/a_s$ and is stable against its counterpropagating competitor when $a_c>a_s$. Equal standing-wave intensities are $r/(a_s+a_c)$; with $r>0$ and positive denominator they are stable to intensity differences when $a_s>a_c$. Phase symmetries give orbital, rather than strict phase-decay, stability. Spatial modulation, mean flows and oblique <convection rolls> require additional stability tests.

= Weakly nonlinear rotating-convection roll stability
{parent=Rotating Rayleigh-Bénard convection}

A saturated <convection roll> is not stable merely because its single-amplitude cubic coefficient is positive. Perturbations include phase sidebands, transverse bending, differently oriented <convection rolls>, oscillatory modes and large-scale <Eulerian mean flow>. Their growth rates follow from the corresponding enlarged <amplitude equations> or the full <linearization> about the <convection roll>. <Küppers–Lortz instability> and <small-angle instability of rotating convection rolls> are distinct orientation-dependent mechanisms.

= Small-angle instability of rotating convection rolls
{parent=Weakly nonlinear rotating-convection roll stability}

For finite <Prandtl number> and stress-free boundaries, nearly parallel <convection rolls> can interact with a weakly damped large-scale <Eulerian mean flow>. The ordinary regular two-roll amplitude expansion becomes nonuniform as the angle tends to zero, so the mean-flow mode must be retained. This mechanism is not automatically the finite-angle <Küppers–Lortz instability>, and thresholds depend on the stated boundary and parameter regime.

= Küppers–Lortz instability
{c}
{parent=Weakly nonlinear rotating-convection roll stability}

For an existing <convection roll> of intensity $r/g_0$, the amplitude of a new orientation at relative angle $\vartheta$ grows at rate $r[1-g(\vartheta)/g_0]$. Rotation permits $g(\vartheta)\neq g(-\vartheta)$. If one oblique orientation has $g(\vartheta)<g_0$, the <convection roll> is unstable immediately above a supercritical stationary threshold. This is the Küppers–Lortz mechanism. Successive orientation replacements can produce time-dependent <convection roll> switching; a universal attractor does not follow from the two-mode calculation. The perturbing orientation requires three-dimensional disturbances even if the initial <convection roll> is two-dimensional.

= Wavenumber selection in rotating convection
{parent=Rotating Rayleigh-Bénard convection}

For the free-slip plane layer, onset selects the smallest <Rayleigh number> across all admissible stationary and oscillatory <normal modes>. The first vertical mode wins because increasing vertical index raises viscous cost and reduces oscillatory admissibility. Oscillatory minimization replaces the right-hand side of the stationary wave-number equation by $P^2\mathrm{Ta}\pi^2/(1+P)^2$. For the oscillatory minimum also require positive <angular frequency> squared. Where both selected curves are admissible, at rapid rotation the ratio of the minimized thresholds is $R_{o,c}/R_{s,c}\sim2P^{4/3}/(1+P)^{1/3}$. Equality gives $8P^4-P-1=0$, with positive root about $0.67660$; below it sufficiently rapid rotation can select oscillatory primary onset.

= Rotating-convection growth-rate polynomial
{parent=Rotating Rayleigh-Bénard convection}

For stress-free impermeable fixed-temperature plates, thermal-time growth rate $s$, horizontal <wavenumber> $k$ and vertical index $n$, put $a=k^2+n^2\pi^2$ and $P=\operatorname{Pr}$. Vertical <velocity>, <temperature> and vertical <vorticity> amplitudes satisfy $a(s/P+a)W+\sqrt{\mathrm{Ta}}n\pi Z=Rk^2\Theta$, $(s/P+a)Z=\sqrt{\mathrm{Ta}}n\pi W$, $(s+a)\Theta=W$. Taking their determinant gives
$$
(s+a)[a(s/P+a)^2+\mathrm{Ta}\,n^2\pi^2]-Rk^2(s/P+a)=0.
$$
This is a cubic and remains valid without dividing by a potentially zero factor. Setting $s=0$ or $s=i\omega$ determines the stationary or admissible oscillatory neutral curves.

= Oscillatory neutral curve of rotating convection
{parent=Rotating-convection growth-rate polynomial}

For $s=i\omega$, real-imaginary separation of the rotating-convection cubic gives
$$
\omega^2=P^2\left[\frac{1-P}{1+P}\frac{\mathrm{Ta}\,n^2\pi^2}{a}-a^2\right],\qquad R_o=\frac{2(1+P)a^3+2P^2\mathrm{Ta}\,n^2\pi^2/(1+P)}{k^2}.
$$
The curve is admissible only if $\omega^2>0$, so $P<1$ and sufficient rotation are necessary. A formal minimum with nonpositive <angular frequency> squared is not a <Hopf bifurcation>. At zero <angular frequency> this curve meets the stationary curve for that fixed <wavenumber> in a double-zero limit.

= Stationary neutral curve of rotating convection
{parent=Rotating-convection growth-rate polynomial}
{title2=$R_s=[(k^2+n^2\pi^2)^3+\mathrm{Ta}\,n^2\pi^2]/k^2$}

Substitution of zero growth rate into the rotating-convection cubic gives the displayed <Rayleigh number>. For the first vertical mode, setting $t=k^2$, $m=\pi^2$, differentiating with respect to $t$ gives $(t+m)^2(2t-m)=\mathrm{Ta}\,m$. At rapid rotation, $k_c\sim(\mathrm{Ta}\pi^2/2)^{1/6}$ and $R_c\sim3(\mathrm{Ta}\pi^2/2)^{2/3}$. Discrete allowed <wavenumbers> or different plate conditions require a different minimization.

= Free-slip convection neutral curve
{parent=Rayleigh-Bénard convection}
{title2=$R_0(k)=(k^2+\pi^2)^3/k^2$}

For nonrotating convection under the <Boussinesq approximation> with fixed-temperature impermeable <stress-free boundary conditions>, a marginal vertical mode $\sin(n\pi z)$ has neutral <Rayleigh number> $(k^2+n^2\pi^2)^3/k^2$. The minimum is at $n=1$, $k=\pi/\sqrt2$, and $R_c=27\pi^4/4$. These follow by eliminating the <temperature> from $(D^2-k^2)^2w=Rk^2\theta$ and $(D^2-k^2)\theta=-w$, then minimizing over $k$.

= Infinite-Prandtl-number convection
{parent=Rayleigh-Bénard convection}
{title2=$\operatorname{Pr}\to\infty$}

In thermal-time units, taking the <Prandtl number> large removes momentum inertia at leading order. The <Stokes flow> momentum equation then determines <velocity> instantaneously from the <temperature> perturbation, while the nonlinear <heat equation> retains its time derivative. Small <Prandtl number> does not generally give this model. An exact contrasting shear in a free-slip layer is $u=U_0e^{-\operatorname{Pr}\pi^2t}\cos\pi z$, $w=\theta=0$: it decays slowly as the <Prandtl number> tends to zero but is excluded by the instantaneous Stokes equation.

= Fastest growth in infinite-Prandtl convection
{parent=Infinite-Prandtl-number convection}
{title2=$\lambda_\infty=Rk^2/(k^2+\pi^2)^2-(k^2+\pi^2)$}

For a stress-free, fixed-temperature layer, the infinite-<Prandtl number> growth rate of the fundamental vertical <normal mode> is $\lambda_\infty=Rk^2/(k^2+\pi^2)^2-(k^2+\pi^2)$. For large <Rayleigh number>, its maximum has $k^2=\pi^2-8\pi^6/R+O(R^{-2})$ and $\lambda_{\rm max}=R/(4\pi^2)-2\pi^2+O(R^{-1})$. The infinite-<Prandtl number> limit must be taken first, or inertia must remain small on the resulting growth-time scale. This optimum differs from the <free-slip convection neutral curve> minimum, which selects onset rather than fastest growth far above it.

= Stokes temperature-slaving operator
{c}
{parent=Infinite-Prandtl-number convection}
{title2=$\mathcal W_R=Rk^2(D^2-k^2)^{-2}$}

For a nonzero horizontal <Fourier mode> $k$, solve $(D^2-k^2)^2w=Rk^2\theta$, $w=w''=0$, to obtain $w=\mathcal W_R\theta$. Under homogeneous <temperature> <Dirichlet boundary conditions>, the sine modes diagonalize this <self-adjoint operator>, with <eigenvalues> $Rk^2/(k^2+n^2\pi^2)^2$. The <temperature> <linear operator> is $\mathcal L_R=D^2-k^2+\mathcal W_R$. At a marginal mode $g=\sin(n\pi z)$ it has $\mathcal W_Rg=(k^2+n^2\pi^2)g$.

= Damped-driven Navier-Stokes equation
{c}
{parent=Navier-Stokes equation}

A damped-driven Navier-Stokes equation adds linear damping and external forcing. In two-dimensional steady vorticity form it reads
$$
-\nu\Delta\omega+\gamma\omega+u\mathbin\cdot\nabla\omega=g,
\qquad \nabla\mathbin\cdot u=0.
$$

= Damped-vorticity maximum estimate
{parent=Damped-driven Navier-Stokes equation}

For a steady damped vorticity equation with $\gamma>0$, divergence-free transport, and bounded forcing, multiplication by $\omega|\omega|^{q-2}$ gives $\gamma\|\omega\|_q\leq\|g\|_q$. Sending $q$ to infinity yields
$$
\gamma\|\omega\|_\infty\leq\|g\|_\infty.
$$

= Vanishing-viscosity limit
{parent=Damped-driven Navier-Stokes equation}
{wiki}

A vanishing-viscosity limit studies solutions as the viscosity coefficient tends to zero. Uniform estimates and compactness can make the viscous term disappear weakly while retaining enough strong convergence to pass nonlinear transport terms to an inviscid equation.

= Pipe flow
{parent=Viscous fluid flow}
{wiki}

Pipe flow is fluid motion through a closed cylindrical conduit. In fully developed axisymmetric flow, the axial velocity depends only on radius and the axial pressure gradient sets a shear stress linear in radius.

= Hagen-Poiseuille equation
{c}
{parent=Pipe flow}
{wiki=Hagen–Poiseuille_equation}

= Hagen-Poiseuille flow
{c}
{synonym}

= Poiseuille flow
{c}
{synonym}

Steady pressure-driven flow through a circular pipe has the parabolic profile
$$
w(r)=-\frac1{4\mu}\frac{dp}{dz}(R^2-r^2).
$$

= Kinetic-energy balance for an incompressible Newtonian fluid
{parent=Viscous fluid flow}

For a fixed domain $\mathcal D$, body-force density $f$, and stress tensor $\sigma$, the kinetic energy satisfies
$$
\frac d{dt}\int_{\mathcal D}\frac12\rho|u|^2dV
+\int_{\partial\mathcal D}\frac12\rho|u|^2u\cdot n\,dS
=\int_{\mathcal D}u\cdot f\,dV
+\int_{\partial\mathcal D}u\cdot\sigma\cdot n\,dS
-2\mu\int_{\mathcal D}e:e\,dV.
$$
The last term is the nonnegative rate at which viscosity converts mechanical energy into heat.

= Dissipation estimate for high-Reynolds-number bubble drag
{parent=Kinetic-energy balance for an incompressible Newtonian fluid}

For a clean bubble of size $a$ and speed $U$ at large <Reynolds number>, the outer <potential flow> violates the stress-free condition by a strain of order $U/a$. A boundary layer of thickness $\delta\sim a\operatorname{Re}^{-1/2}$ needs a velocity correction only of order $U\delta/a$, so its dissipation is smaller than the outer-flow dissipation by order $\delta/a$. The leading drag power is therefore obtained from
$$
DU\sim 2\mu\int_{\rm outer}e:e\,dV.
$$

= Drag on a two-dimensional circular bubble from outer-flow dissipation
{title2=$D=8\pi\mu U$}
{parent=Dissipation estimate for high-Reynolds-number bubble drag}

For the zero-circulation flow around a circle, $e:e=8U^2a^4/r^6$. Integration over the exterior plane gives dissipation $8\pi\mu U^2$ per unit axial length and hence drag $D=8\pi\mu U$ per unit length.

\Include[stokes-flow]{parent=Viscous fluid flow}

= Viscous sheet
{parent=Viscous fluid flow}

A viscous sheet is a thin fluid layer whose motion is controlled by <viscous fluid flow> and whose two broad faces can move freely. Unlike a no-slip wall film, an extensional sheet with shear-free faces has leading tangential velocity nearly uniform across its thickness. The in-plane <stresses> depend on extension in both horizontal directions.

= Planar viscous-sheet stretching equations
{parent=Viscous sheet}
{title2=$h_t+(hu)_x=0,\quad(4\mu h u_x)_x+\gamma h h_{xxx}/2=0$}

A symmetric thin <viscous sheet> with shear-free faces has leading longitudinal <velocity> independent of thickness coordinate $z$, and <incompressibility> gives $w=-zu_x$. <Young–Laplace equation> and the <Newtonian fluid stress tensor> give $\sigma_{zz}=-p_a+\gamma h_{xx}/2$ and $\sigma_{xx}=-p_a+\gamma h_{xx}/2+4\mu u_x$. The effective longitudinal tension per unit transverse width is $\mathcal T=4\mu h u_x+\gamma hh_{xx}/2-\gamma h_x^2/4+2\gamma$. The last two terms include the horizontal projections of the two <surface tension> pulls. <Force balance> gives $\mathcal T_x=0$, yielding the displayed longitudinal equation; the face kinematic conditions give the displayed <conservation of mass> equation.

= Capillary drainage of a film between two bubbles
{parent=Planar viscous-sheet stretching equations}
{title2=$U=\frac{3\gamma}{8\mu}\sqrt{h_0/a},\quad h_0\propto t^{-2}$}

Two cylindrical bubbles separated by a uniform flat liquid film of half-length $L$ drain through short curvature-transition regions at the ends. The elevated gas pressure implies a film liquid pressure approximately $\gamma/a$ above the outer liquid pressure. The transition length is $\delta\sim\sqrt{ah_0}$, and its <volume flux> is nearly constant because $\delta/L\ll1$. Write $uh=Uh_0$ and $\xi=(x-L)/\delta$. The <planar viscous-sheet stretching equations> reduce to $hh'''=C(\log h)''$, with $C=8\mu Uh_0\delta/\gamma$. Two integrations, matched to a flat film at the inner end, give $h'/\sqrt h=(16\mu U\sqrt a/(3\gamma))[1-(h_0/h)^{3/2}]$. Matching the curvature to $1/a$ gives the displayed speed. Uniform-film <conservation of mass> then gives $\dot h_0=-Uh_0/L$, hence $h_0=[h_i^{-1/2}+3\gamma t/(16\mu L\sqrt a)]^{-2}$. There is no finite-time contact in this ideal continuum model.

= Axisymmetric viscous-sheet stretching equations
{parent=Viscous sheet}

For thickness $h(r,t)$, radial velocity $u(r,t)$ and external pressure $p_{\rm ext}$, the leading <Newtonian fluid stress tensor> and <incompressibility> give
$$
\sigma_{rr}=-p_{\rm ext}+2\mu(2u_r+u/r),\qquad
\sigma_{\theta\theta}=-p_{\rm ext}+2\mu(u_r+2u/r).
$$
Radial <force balance> on an annular sector includes the inward projection of hoop <traction> and external pressure on the sloping broad faces. Together with <conservation of mass> it yields
$$
2\mu\left[\partial_r(2rhu_r+hu)-h(2u/r+u_r)\right]=rh\,\partial_rp_{\rm ext},\qquad
h_t+r^{-1}\partial_r(rhu)=0.
$$

= Self-similar spreading of a viscous oil slick
{parent=Axisymmetric viscous-sheet stretching equations}

For external pressure $\Delta\rho\,gh$ and edge condition $h\sigma_{rr}=-\Delta\rho\,gh^2/2$, the fixed-volume <similarity solution> is a uniformly thick disc:
$$
h=\frac{6\mu}{\Delta\rho\,gt},\qquad u=\frac r{2t},\qquad R^2=\frac{\Delta\rho\,gVt}{6\pi\mu}.
$$
<Conservation of mass> fixes the similarity velocity. The radial equation then forces a differentiable thickness profile to be constant, and the edge <stress> fixes that constant. The singular point-release solution describes the spreading scaling; a finite initial uniform disc corresponds to shifting time.

= Capillary growth of a hole in a viscous sheet
{parent=Axisymmetric viscous-sheet stretching equations}

For a uniform annular sheet attached to a fixed outer rim $R_0$, two <surface tension> pulls give $h\sigma_{rr}=-2\gamma$ at the hole radius $R$. The zero-pressure radial equation gives $u=Ar+B/r$, with $B=-AR_0^2$ and $A=-\gamma R^2/[\mu h(R_0^2+3R^2)]$. The thickness remains uniform by <conservation of mass>. With negligible initial hole volume, $h=h_0/(1-x^2)$ and
$$
\dot x=\frac\gamma{\mu h_0}\frac{x(1-x^2)^2}{1+3x^2},\qquad x=R/R_0.
$$
A positive seed hole grows initially exponentially. An exactly zero initial radius remains zero within this continuum evolution law.

= Lubrication theory
{parent=Viscous fluid flow}
{wiki}

= Lubrication approximation
{synonym}

= Lubrication
{synonym}

Lubrication theory describes viscous flow through a gap whose thickness is much smaller than its streamwise length; pressure is nearly uniform across the gap and controls a locally parabolic velocity profile.

= Gravity-driven spreading of a planar viscous drop
{parent=Lubrication theory}
{title2=$\ell(t)=[5gA^3t/(36\nu I^3)]^{1/5}$}

A wide, shallow drop with cross-sectional area $A$ has the gravity-driven <volume flux> $q=-gh^3h_x/(3\nu)$ when substrate no-slip and zero surface shear are imposed. <Mass conservation> yields a <porous medium equation>, $h_t=[g/(3\nu)](h^3h_x)_x$. Its symmetric source-type <similarity solution> has half-width $\ell$ in the display and height $h=[9\nu(\ell^2-x^2)/(10gt)]_+^{1/3}$, where $I=\int_0^{\pi/2}\cos^{5/3}\theta\,d\theta$. Integrating the profile recovers $A$. The contact-edge slope diverges, so the bulk <lubrication approximation> requires an inner edge regularization if contact-line microphysics or <surface tension> is retained.

= Capillary instability of a thin film on a rigid cylinder
{parent=Lubrication theory}
{title2=$h_t+[h^3(h_z+h_{zzz})]_z=0$}

A thin axisymmetric coating of physical thickness $d=\epsilon a h$ on a rigid cylinder of radius $a$ has leading curvature $a^{-1}-d/a^2-d_{\bar z\bar z}$. With constant <surface tension> $\gamma$, negligible <Newtonian gravity>, <no-slip boundary condition> at the wall and negligible exterior stress, <lubrication theory> gives $q=-d^3p_{\bar z}/(3\mu)$. Set $z=\bar z/a$ and $t=\gamma\epsilon^3\bar t/(3\mu a)$ to obtain the displayed <thin-film equation>. A perturbation of a uniform film $h_0$ proportional to $e^{st+ikz}$ has
$$
s=h_0^3(k^2-k^4).
$$
Thus the fastest <Rayleigh–Plateau instability> has $|k|=1/\sqrt2$, or physical wavelength $2\pi\sqrt2a$.

= Volume-constrained capillary energy of a cylindrical coating
{parent=Capillary instability of a thin film on a rigid cylinder}
{title2=$\mathcal E=\tfrac12\int(h_z^2-h^2)\,dz$}

Expand the area of an axisymmetric cylindrical free surface to second order in film thickness and eliminate the first-order area term using conservation of the exact liquid volume. Up to positive factors and constants this gives the displayed <surface energy>. Its variation is $\delta\mathcal E/\delta h=-h-h_{zz}$. The <thin-film equation> is the conserved <gradient flow> $h_t=\partial_z[h^3\partial_z(\delta\mathcal E/\delta h)]$. For periodic boundaries, or boundaries with vanishing energy flux,
$$
\frac{d\mathcal E}{dt}=-\int h^3(h_z+h_{zzz})^2\,dz\le0.
$$
The decrease in capillary <surface energy> supplies <viscous dissipation>. On an infinite coating with unequal far films, use a local energy balance including boundary fluxes rather than an unsubtracted infinite energy integral.

= Quasisteady sliding collar on a coated cylinder
{parent=Capillary instability of a thin film on a rigid cylinder}

A large collar translating slowly at speed $c$ over a film $h_+$ and leaving a thinner film $h_-$ must grow: its leading excess volume increases at rate $c(h_+-h_-)$. An exact <travelling wave> connecting unequal stationary films is impossible, since integration of the <thin-film equation> would give a common constant $q-ch=-ch_+=-ch_-$. In a slowly evolving collar core the leading <pressure> gradient vanishes. Therefore $h'+h'''=0$, and a centred, reflection-symmetric collar has $h=A(1+\cos x)+B$ on $-\pi<x<\pi$. Its leading volume is $2\pi A$, so for constant small $B$, $2\pi\dot A\simeq c(h_+-h_-)$. The speed is supplied by <collar-to-film capillary matching>.

= Collar-to-film capillary matching
{parent=Quasisteady sliding collar on a coated cylinder}
{title2=$H^3H'''=H-1$}

Near a collar edge adjoining a film of thickness $h_f$, local <mass conservation> gives $h^3h'''=c(h-h_f)$. The scaling $h=h_fH$, $x-x_f=h_fX/c^{1/3}$ yields the displayed equation. Its linearization at $H=1$ has exponents $1$ and $-1/2\pm i\sqrt3/2$. The positive growing rear branch approaching one as $X\to-\infty$ has one amplitude, removable by translation; the front's decaying oscillatory branches have two amplitudes, leaving one parameter after translation. This dimension count describes local branches; global positivity and selection require matching. If their core asymptotes, after removing linear terms by translation, are $H_-\sim a_-X^2+b_-$ and $H_+\sim a_+(\lambda)X^2+b_+(\lambda)$, matching to the collar gives $A/2=a_\pm c^{2/3}/h_\pm$ and $B=b_\pm h_\pm$. Consequently $a_+b_+=a_-b_-$; a selected root with $\alpha=a_+(\lambda_0)>a_-$ yields $c=(Ah_+/(2\alpha))^{3/2}$, $B=h_+b_+(\lambda_0)$ and $h_-/h_+=a_-/\alpha$. Local linearization alone does not establish uniqueness of this global matching root.

= Cylinder translating parallel to a wall in a thin gap
{parent=Lubrication theory}
{title2=$F=2\pi\mu V\sqrt{2a/b}$}

For a nonrotating cylinder of radius $a$ translating parallel to a stationary wall at minimum gap $b\ll a$, the leading gap is $h=b+x^2/(2a)$. In the cylinder frame, the constant flux is $-2Vb/3$ and the pressure gradient is $2\mu V(4b-3h)/h^3$. The pressure is $2\mu Vx/h^2$. Integrating the wall shear $4\mu V(1/h-b/h^2)$ gives the displayed leading force per unit axial length. Full wetting and negligible inertia are assumed; cavitation would change the pressure boundary conditions.

= Cusp flow between touching cylinders
{parent=Lubrication theory}
{title2=$\mathcal A=w^3/(3a),\quad\kappa=2a/w^2$}

On one side of two touching cylinders of radius $a$, the leading gap is $d(x)=x^2/a$. A completely wetting semicircular meniscus at $x=w\ll a$ has radius $w^2/(2a)$ and suction $p=-2\gamma a/w^2$. The area is $\mathcal A=\int_0^wd(x)dx=w^3/(3a)$ to leading order. Integrating the local slit <Poiseuille flow> gives $Q=-w^7p_z/(84\mu a^3)=-\gamma w^4w_z/(21\mu a^2)$. Thus $(w^3)_t=\gamma(w^4w_z)_z/(7\mu a)$.

= Hydrostatic rise in a cylindrical cusp
{parent=Cusp flow between touching cylinders}
{title2=$w(z)\sim[2\gamma a/(\rho gz)]^{1/2}$}

For vertical touching cylinders in a bath, the hydrostatic suction at height $z$ is $\rho gz$. Equating it to the meniscus suction $2\gamma a/w^2$ gives the displayed large-height width. Ideal perfect wetting permits an arbitrarily high thin cusp tail, unlike a uniform finite-gap capillary. The prediction assumes the continuum model remains valid as the gap narrows.

= Fixed-volume capillary spreading in a cylindrical cusp
{parent=Cusp flow between touching cylinders}
{title2=$w=[21\mu a(z_N^2-z^2)/(8\gamma t)]_+^{1/2}$}

For fixed volume $V$ and symmetric spreading, <conservation of mass> and the <cusp flow between touching cylinders> equation give width proportional to $t^{-1/8}$ and support length proportional to $t^{3/8}$. The source-type <similarity solution> has the displayed profile and $z_N=(8Va/\pi)^{1/4}(8\gamma t/(21\mu a))^{3/8}$. It is a formal outer lubrication solution; its microscopic nose requires a local description when the axial slope becomes large.

= Lubrication model of a crawling snail
{parent=Lubrication theory}
{title2=$U/V=6(1-\alpha)/(4-3\alpha),\quad\alpha=I_2^2/(I_1I_3)$}

= Snail locomotion by lubrication flow
{synonym}

For a periodic thin mucus gap $h(x)$ translating as a backward wave relative to a force-free foot, put $I_j=\int_0^Lh^{-j}dx$. In the wave frame, the plane and foot have horizontal <velocities> $V-U$ and $V$. A <Couette-Poiseuille flow> with periodic <pressure> has flux $q=(V-U/2)I_2/I_3$. Zero net plane <force> gives the displayed crawling speed. The leading <viscous dissipation> per wavelength is $\Phi=2\mu UVI_1$.

= Optimal sinusoidal waveform for viscous snail locomotion
{parent=Lubrication model of a crawling snail}
{title2=$A=1/\sqrt2,\quad V=4U/3,\quad\Phi_{\min}=(8\sqrt2/3)\mu LU^2/h_0$}

For $h=h_0[1-A\sin(2\pi x/L)]$, the crawling speed is $U/V=3A^2/(1+2A^2)$. At prescribed $U$, eliminate $V$ to obtain $\Phi=2\mu LU^2(1+2A^2)/(3h_0A^2\sqrt{1-A^2})$. The derivative in $A^2$ vanishes only at $A^2=1/2$, and the dissipation diverges at both endpoints. The displayed optimum still dissipates $8\sqrt2/3$ times the simple <Couette flow> dissipation of an externally driven flat foot moving at speed $U$ across a uniform film of thickness $h_0$.

= Rotating roller under a nearly flat free surface
{parent=Lubrication theory}
{title2=$Q=4Ub/3$}

A rotating roller with top speed $U$ and gap $d(x)=b+x^2/(2a)$ drives a shear-free <lubrication approximation> flow. No slip below and zero shear above give $u=U+p_x(y^2-d^2)/(2\mu)$ and $Q=Ud-d^3p_x/(3\mu)$. The <pressure recovery condition in lubrication flow> then gives $Q=U\int d^{-2}dx/\int d^{-3}dx=4Ub/3$. With $s=x/\sqrt{2ab}$, the excess pressure is $-\mu U\sqrt{2a}\,b^{-3/2}s/(1+s^2)^2$. Gravity supports it through the small surface displacement $h=(p-p_0)/(\rho g)$; the flat leading geometry is consistent when $\mu|U|\sqrt a/(\rho g b^{5/2})\ll1$.

= Lubrication resistance
{parent=Lubrication theory}
{title2=$F\sim\mu a^2 U/h$}

<Lubrication theory> predicts large viscous resistance when fluid must be squeezed through a narrow gap. For normal approach of smooth curved surfaces with curvature scale $a$ and minimum separation $h\ll a$, the resisting <force> scales as $\mu a^2U/h$. Under a bounded normal <force> this gives $|\dot h|=O(h)$, so ideal smooth surfaces do not make contact in finite time. Tangential and rolling motions have different resistance laws; the normal squeeze law should not be applied to them.

= Axial rotational resistance of eccentric nested spheres
{parent=Lubrication resistance}
{title2=$\mathcal R_\Omega=\frac{2\pi\mu a^4}{\Delta\alpha^3}[2\alpha+(1-\alpha^2)\log\frac{1-\alpha}{1+\alpha}]$}

Rotation about the offset axis has azimuthal <Couette flow> and no pressure variation at leading <lubrication> order. Integrating the tangential traction gives torque $-\mathcal R_\Omega\Omega$ on the inner sphere. The concentric limit is $8\pi\mu a^4/(3\Delta)$ and the contact limit is finite, $4\pi\mu a^4/\Delta$, because the wall velocity vanishes at the closest pole.

= Squeeze resistance of eccentric nested spheres
{parent=Lubrication resistance}
{title2=$\mathcal R=\frac{6\pi\mu a^4}{\Delta^3\alpha^3}[\frac{2\alpha}{1-\alpha^2}+\log\frac{1-\alpha}{1+\alpha}]$}

For an inner sphere of radius $a$, outer radius $a+\Delta$, and signed offset $\alpha\Delta$ along a fixed axis, the leading <lubrication pressure> generated by offset speed $V=\Delta\dot\alpha$ is $p=3\mu a^2V/[\alpha\Delta^3(1-\alpha\cos\theta)^2]+\text{constant}$. Its force on the inner sphere is $-\mathcal R V$ along that axis. Remove the spatially constant divergent term before taking $\alpha\to0$; the resistance then tends to $8\pi\mu a^4/\Delta^3$. As $\alpha\to1$, it is $6\pi\mu a^4/[\Delta^3(1-\alpha)]$ to leading order.

= Lubrication prevents finite-time collision of smooth spheres
{parent=Lubrication resistance}
{title2=$F_n\sim\mu a^2(-\dot\delta)/\delta$}

For smooth no-slip <spheres> of radius scale $a$ with minimum gap $\delta\ll a$, the lateral gap scale is $(a\delta)^{1/2}$. Squeeze-film <lubrication theory> gives <pressure> $p\sim\mu a(-\dot\delta)/\delta^2$ and the displayed normal resistance. Bounded forcing therefore gives $-\dot\delta=O(\delta)$, so the gap cannot vanish in finite time. The conclusion uses smooth surfaces, no slip and continuum creeping flow; it is not a contact model for rough particles.

= Viscous mountain spreading over an underthrust plate
{parent=Lubrication theory}
{title2=$q=Uh-\rho gh^3h_x/(3\mu)$}

For a Newtonian layer with density $\rho$, <dynamic viscosity> $\mu$, thickness $h$ and a plate moving at $U$, a stress-free roof and no-slip base give $q=Uh-\rho gh^3h_x/(3\mu)$. Zero net mountain-material <volume flux> produces $h^3=9\mu Ux/(\rho g)$ when the contact height vanishes. Very near its idealized contact line the slope violates the <lubrication approximation>.

= Sediment-lubricated viscous mountain model
{parent=Viscous mountain spreading over an underthrust plate}
{title2=$q_s,\ q_m$}

A lower-viscosity basal sediment layer changes the plate coupling in <viscous mountain spreading over an underthrust plate>. With mountain thickness $H$ and sediment depth $d$, equal densities give one hydrostatic pressure gradient $G=\rho g\partial_x(H+d)$. Fluxes are $q_s=Ud-G(d^2H/2+d^3/3)/\mu_s$ and $q_m=UH-G(dH^2+d^2H/2)/\mu_s-GH^3/(3\mu)$. Separate <conservation of mass> laws determine each layer. Steady zero mountain flux and sediment influx $Ud_\infty$ give contact height $3d_\infty$ and surface slope $2\mu_sU/(9\rho gd_\infty^2)$.

= Upstream basin profile of a sediment-lubricated mountain
{parent=Sediment-lubricated viscous mountain model}
{title2=$x/\ell=P(d/d_\infty)-P(3)$}

In front of the contact line in the <sediment-lubricated viscous mountain model>, $h=d$ and $d_x=3\mu_sU(d-d_\infty)/(\rho gd^3)$. For $\delta=d/d_\infty$ and $\ell=\rho gd_\infty^3/(3\mu_sU)$, integration gives $x/\ell=P(\delta)-P(3)$, with $P(\delta)=\delta^3/3+\delta^2/2+\delta+\ln(\delta-1)$. The branch $1<\delta\leq3$ is unique and tends exponentially to the uniform far-field depth.

= Squeegee lubrication model
{parent=Lubrication theory}
{title2=$h_t-h_x=\nabla\cdot(h^3\nabla h)/3$}

A blade moving over a thin gravity-driven viscous film produces a moving-frame flux $\mathbf q=-h\mathbf e_x-h^3\nabla h/3$. Scaling film thickness by $h_0$ and horizontal distance by $\rho gh_0^3/(\mu U)$ yields this dimensionless volume-conservation equation. Fluid can escape beneath the blade or around its ends.

= Finite-blade side-drainage pile
{parent=Squeegee lubrication model}
{title2=$h(0^+,y)=[126(\alpha^2-y^2)]^{1/7}$}

A long blade with negligible under-blade leakage diverts incoming fluid laterally. A deep-pile profile $h^3=9[x_N(y)-x]$ and integrated flux conservation give $d(\int h^4dx)/dy=-12y$, hence $x_N=[126(\alpha^2-y^2)]^{3/7}/9$. This leading approximation excludes the nose and blade-tip transition regions.

= Squeegee gap leakage flux
{parent=Squeegee lubrication model}
{title2=$J\simeq2\epsilon/3+2\epsilon^{5/2}\Delta p/(9\pi\sqrt{2\delta})$}

For a rounded blade with local <parabolic lubrication gap> $d=\epsilon+x^2/(2\delta)$, $\epsilon\ll\delta$, the <Couette-Poiseuille flow in a thin gap> yields a floor-driven flux $2\epsilon/3$ and pressure-driven leakage proportional to $\epsilon^{5/2}\Delta p/\sqrt\delta$. Positive $J$ is from the upstream pile towards the rear.

= Infinite-blade gravity pile
{parent=Squeegee lubrication model}
{title2=$X(h)=x_0-x$}

The steady upstream film of an infinite blade has $h_x=-3(h-1)/h^3$. Thus $X(h)=h^3/9+h^2/6+h/3+\log(h-1)/3$. The head is fixed by under-blade leakage balancing the incoming film. The deep pile has a cubic profile, while the actual upstream transition has an exponential tail towards $h=1$.

= Journal bearing
{parent=Lubrication theory}

A journal bearing supports a rotating axle inside a surrounding cylindrical sleeve with a thin lubricating film. An eccentric gap generates <lubrication pressure> as rotation transports fluid into and out of narrower regions. A <full-film journal bearing> model imposes periodic <pressure> around the entire circumference; a model with <cavitation> needs additional free-boundary conditions. The two models can predict different loads and dynamics.

= Full-film journal bearing
{parent=Journal bearing}

A full-film journal bearing model assumes that liquid fills the entire annular gap and that <pressure> is single-valued around a complete circumference. No <cavitation> boundary is imposed. The <full-film eccentric journal-bearing rotation> and <full-film journal-bearing whirl> formulas use this assumption; their predicted motion depends on keeping it.

= Full-film journal-bearing whirl
{parent=Journal bearing}

A freely translating inner axle with prescribed positive <angular velocity> $\Omega$ and downward load $F>0$ has <full-film journal bearing> <force balance> equations
$$
R_r\dot r=-F\sin\phi,\qquad R_t r(\dot\phi-\Omega/2)=-F\cos\phi,
$$
where $R_r,R_t$ are the <squeeze resistance of an eccentric journal bearing>. Rotation supplies tangential fluid <force> $R_t\Omega r/2$. The equilibrium lies horizontally to the right and satisfies $F=R_t\Omega r/2$. <Linearization> there has imaginary <eigenvalues>: the prescribed rotating axle continuously supplies energy, so viscous dissipation does not imply settling. For an initially concentric axle, putting $e=r/\Delta$ and $K=6\pi\mu\Omega a^3/\Delta^2$ gives the closed orbit
$$
\frac x\Delta=\frac{2K}{5F}\left[(1-e^2)^{-1/2}-(1-e^2)^{3/4}\right],\qquad z^2=\Delta^2e^2-x^2.
$$
This follows by eliminating time and integrating $x_e+3ex/[2(1-e^2)]=\Omega\Delta^2R_0e/[2F(1-e^2)^{3/2}]$. It applies to the leading inertia-free full-film model; adding <cavitation>, constraints or finite-length leakage changes the prediction.

= Squeeze resistance of an eccentric journal bearing
{parent=Journal bearing}
{title2=$R_r,\ R_t$}

Let the inner-centre displacement from a fixed outer centre have magnitude $r=\Delta e$, with $0\leq e<1$. The leading <full-film journal bearing> resistance to translation in the displacement direction and perpendicular to it is
$$
R_r=\frac{12\pi\mu a^3}{\Delta^3(1-e^2)^{3/2}},\qquad
R_t=\frac{12\pi\mu a^3}{\Delta^3\sqrt{1-e^2}(1+e^2/2)}.
$$
For $h=\Delta(1-e\cos\theta)$, the <Reynolds lubrication equation> gives radial squeezing flux $av_r\sin\theta$ and tangential squeezing flux $av_t[-\cos\theta+3e/(2+e^2)]$. The additive constant enforces periodic <pressure>. Integrating the <pressure> traction gives these coefficients; at $e=0$ they coincide, as required by <rotational symmetry>.

= Full-film eccentric journal-bearing rotation
{parent=Journal bearing}

For inner radius $a$, mean gap $\Delta\ll a$, fixed eccentricity parameter $|\alpha|<1$, gap $h=\Delta(1+\alpha\sin\theta)$, and inner <angular velocity> $\Omega$, the constant circumferential flux is
$$
q=\frac{\Omega a\Delta}{2}\frac{1-\alpha^2}{1+\alpha^2/2}.
$$
It follows by integrating the <lubrication theory> relation $p_s=6\mu\Omega a/h^2-12\mu q/h^3$ over a full period. The leading holding <force> is horizontal:
$$
F_x=-\frac{6\pi\mu\Omega a^3}{\Delta^2}\frac{\alpha}{\sqrt{1-\alpha^2}(1+\alpha^2/2)},\qquad F_z=0.
$$
<Integration by parts> computes this <force> without a closed-form <pressure>. The fluid couples about the separate cylinder axes obey $C_i+C_o=\alpha\Delta F_x$; moments about a common axis balance exactly at this order.

= Lubrication gravity-current flux
{parent=Lubrication theory}
{title2=$q=-(g/3\nu)h^3h_x$}

For a thin viscous <gravity current> with <hydrostatic pressure> $p=\rho g(h-z)$, a <no-slip boundary condition> at its base and a <stress-free boundary condition> at its top, horizontal momentum balance gives $u=(g/\nu)h_x(z^2/2-hz)$. Integrating across the depth gives the <volume flux> $q=-(g/3\nu)h^3h_x$. Horizontal spreading follows $h_t+q_x=0$ if the substrate is impermeable.

= Draining viscous film on a finite horizontal plate
{parent=Lubrication theory}

With $K=\rho g/(3\mu)$ and formal edge conditions $h(\pm L,t)=0$, the <lubrication theory> equation is $h_t=K(h^3h_x)_x$. Its positive separated long-time profile has
$$
h(x,t)=\left(\frac{L^2}{Kt}\right)^{1/3}H(x/L),\qquad
(H^3H')'=-\frac H3.
$$
Writing $C=B(4/5,1/2)$ in terms of the <beta function>, the centre height is $h_0^3=10\mu L^2/(\rho gC^2t)$. The <edge region of a draining viscous film> determines how the singular outer profile matches the flow over the physical edge.

= Edge region of a draining viscous film
{parent=Draining viscous film on a finite horizontal plate}

The outer <similarity solution> approaches a plate edge as $h/h_0\sim[(4C/5)(1-x/L)]^{1/4}$, where $C=B(4/5,1/2)$. Its slope becomes order one at
$$
\ell\sim L(h_0/L)^{4/3}.
$$
This region lies outside <lubrication theory> because its vertical and horizontal scales are comparable. The outer zero-thickness boundary condition is consistent at leading order since the matching height is $O(\ell)\ll h_0$, while the actual edge flow requires an inner solution. A finite outward flux survives even though the outer thickness vanishes, because the slope diverges.

= Lubrication pressure
{parent=Lubrication theory}

Lubrication pressure is the pressure variation required to drive viscous flow through a thin gap. Its characteristic scale is $P\sim\mu UL/H^2$ for velocity scale $U$, gap thickness $H$, and streamwise length $L$.

= Hele–Shaw flow
{c}
{parent=Lubrication theory}
{wiki=Hele-Shaw_flow}

Hele–Shaw flow is slow flow between closely spaced parallel plates. Averaging across a gap of thickness $h$ gives the Darcy-type law
$$
\overline{\mathbf u}=-\frac{h^2}{12\mu}\nabla p
$$
relative to stationary plates.

= Long-wave approximation
{parent=Lubrication theory}

= Thin-film approximation
{synonym}

A long-wave approximation assumes that a layer thickness $H$ is much smaller than its streamwise length $L$. The small aspect ratio $H/L$ makes transverse gradients dominate longitudinal gradients and is the geometric basis of <lubrication theory>.

= Lubrication pressure dominates shear stress
{parent=Lubrication theory}

For velocity scale $U$, gap thickness $H$, and streamwise length $L\gg H$, lubrication balance gives $P\sim\mu UL/H^2$ and $\tau\sim\mu U/H$. Hence
$$
\frac\tau P\sim\frac HL\ll1.
$$

= Lubrication-limit scaling for a moving thin gap
{parent=Lubrication theory}

For gap scale $H$, length $L$, lower-wall tangential speed $U$, upper-wall normal speed $V$, and induced horizontal scale $W=|U|+VL/H$, the lubrication limit requires
$$
\frac HL\ll1,
\qquad |h'|\ll1,
\qquad
\frac{\rho W H^2}{\mu L}\ll1.
$$
The last condition includes both $\rho|U|H^2/(\mu L)\ll1$ and $\rho VH/\mu\ll1$.

= Porous-plate lubrication cushion
{parent=Lubrication theory}

Uniform normal injection of speed $V$ through the lower plate of a stationary axisymmetric gap of thickness $h$ and radius $R$ produces
$$
w=V\left[1-3(z/h)^2+2(z/h)^3\right],
\qquad
u_r=\frac{3Vr}{h}(z/h)(1-z/h).
$$
With ambient pressure at the rim, the <lubrication pressure> is $p-p_0=3\mu V(R^2-r^2)/h^3$, and its integral supports the load $3\pi\mu VR^4/(2h^3)$.

= Gravity-driven thin film on an incline
{parent=Lubrication theory}

For a film of thickness $h(x,t)$ flowing down a plane inclined by $\alpha$, lubrication theory gives the flux
$$
q=\frac{\rho gh^3}{3\mu}
\left(\sin\alpha-\cos\alpha\,h_x\right)
$$
when surface tension is neglected. Mass conservation gives the thin-film equation $h_t+q_x=0$.

= Inertia criterion for an inclined viscous film
{parent=Gravity-driven thin film on an incline}
{title2=$UH^2/(\nu L)\ll1$}

For a film of thickness $H$, axial length $L\gg H$, and <velocity> $U$, the ratio of convective inertia to transverse viscous stress in <lubrication theory> is $UH^2/(\nu L)$. On a small incline of slope $\theta$, <lubrication gravity-current flux> gives $U\sim g\theta H^2/\nu$ when $H\ll\theta L$, and $U\sim gH^3/(\nu L)$ when $H\gg\theta L$. The respective criteria are $g\theta H^4/(\nu^2L)\ll1$ and $gH^5/(\nu^2L^2)\ll1$. Unsteady inertia also requires $H^2/(\nu T)\ll1$ if the evolution timescale $T$ is independent of $L/U$.

= Travelling front of a gravity-driven thin film
{parent=Gravity-driven thin film on an incline}

For $h_t=\partial_x(-Ah^3+Bh^3h_x)$, $A,B>0$, a front $h=F(x-ct)$ joining $h_0$ behind it to zero at its tip has $c=Ah_0^2$. Integrating once gives $F'=(A/B)(1-h_0^2/F^2)$ and
$$
\xi=\frac BA\left[F+\frac{h_0}{2}\log\frac{h_0-F}{h_0+F}\right].
$$
Near the tip, $F\sim(-3c\xi/B)^{1/3}$. Its unbounded slope signals the failure of the small-slope <lubrication approximation> sufficiently close to the <contact line>.

= Small-slope breakdown at an inclined-current nose
{parent=Travelling front of a gravity-driven thin film}

For a gravity-current nose of height $h_N$ on a small incline of slope $\theta$, the <travelling front of a gravity-driven thin film> satisfies $h_y=\theta(1-h_N^2/h^2)$. Close to its dry tip, $h\sim(-3\theta h_N^2y)^{1/3}$. The slope becomes order one at $h\sim\sqrt\theta h_N$, giving an $O(\sqrt\theta h_N)$ region where <lubrication theory> fails. The main nose length is $h_N/\theta$. Comparing capillary <pressure> gradients $\gamma h_N/(h_N/\theta)^3$ with gravitational forcing $\rho g\theta$ gives the crossover $h_N\sim\theta\sqrt{\gamma/(\rho g)}$. This concerns the main nose length; capillarity may already matter in a much smaller tip region.

= Viscous gravity current on a cone
{parent=Gravity-driven thin film on an incline}

Let $x$ be slant distance from an upward apex, and let the surface slope down at angle $\alpha$ to the horizontal. Axisymmetric <lubrication theory> gives
$$
h_t+\frac Kx\partial_x\left[xh^3(\sin\alpha-\cos\alpha\,h_x)\right]=0,
\qquad K=\frac{\rho g}{3\mu}.
$$
For conserved volume $V$ and $x_N^3\gg V/\sin\alpha$, the leading <similarity solution> has $h=\sqrt{x/(5K\sin\alpha\,t)}$ in $0<x<x_N$, with
$$
x_N^5=\frac{125K\sin\alpha\,V^2t}{16\pi^2\cos^2\alpha}.
$$
The outer front is a discontinuity in this reduced conservation law; the hydrostatic-gradient term and the full local flow resolve the front and apex regions where the outer approximation fails.

= Finite-volume gravity-driven thin-film current
{parent=Gravity-driven thin film on an incline}

For
$$
h_t+(Ch^3)_x=0,\qquad C>0,
$$
a current of conserved area $V$ has the first-kind similarity solution
$$
h(x,t)=\sqrt{\frac{x}{3Ct}},
\qquad 0<x<x_N(t),
$$
terminated by a shock at
$$
x_N(t)=\left(\frac{27CV^2t}{4}\right)^{1/3}.
$$
Its front height is $h_N(t)=(V/(2Ct))^{1/3}$.

= Constant-head supply for a gravity-driven thin film
{parent=Finite-volume gravity-driven thin-film current}

The scaling $x\sim Ch^2t$ and $V\sim hx$ gives $V\sim Ch^3t$. Maintaining an asymptotically constant front height therefore requires constant volume flux of order $Ch^3$.

= Parabolic lubrication gap
{parent=Lubrication theory}

Near the closest approach of a circle of radius $a$ to a wall, the gap is $h(x)=h_0+x^2/(2a)+O(x^4/a^3)$ and has streamwise scale $\sqrt{ah_0}$.

= Pressure-driven flux through a parabolic lubrication gap
{parent=Parabolic lubrication gap}
{title2=$\Delta p=9\pi\mu q\sqrt{2a}/(2h^{5/2})$}

Two stationary no-slip surfaces with local gap $H(x)=h+x^2/(2a)$ transmit pressure-driven <volume flux> $q=-H^3p_x/(12\mu)$ per unit transverse length. Hence the <pressure> drop is
$$
\Delta p=12\mu q\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{dx}{H^3}=\frac{9\pi\mu q\sqrt{2a}}{2h^{5/2}}.
$$
The first relation comes from integrating the parabolic <velocity> $u=-p_x y(H-y)/(2\mu)$. Substitution $x=\sqrt{2ah}\tan\theta$ evaluates the resistance <integral> using $\int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}\cos^4\theta\,d\theta=3\pi/8$. The formula gives the leading resistance when $h\ll a$.

= Lubrication resistance integrals for a nearly occluding sphere
{parent=Parabolic lubrication gap}
{title2=$I_n=\int h^{-n}\,dz$}

A <sphere> almost filling a tube has local <parabolic lubrication gap> $h\simeq a[\epsilon+(z/a)^2]/2$. The resistance integrals are $I_1=2\pi\epsilon^{-1/2}$, $I_2=(2\pi/a)\epsilon^{-3/2}$ and $I_3=(3\pi/a^2)\epsilon^{-5/2}$, obtained with $z=a\sqrt\epsilon\,\xi$ and $\int(1+\xi^2)^{-n}d\xi$. <Moving-boundary lubrication flux> weights shear-driven and pressure-driven transport by different powers of the gap, explaining the three distinct divergence rates.

= Settling speed of a nearly occluding sphere without through-flow
{parent=Lubrication resistance integrals for a nearly occluding sphere}
{title2=$U\sim8\sqrt2\Delta\rho g d^{5/2}/(27\pi\mu\sqrt R)$}

A <sphere> of radius $R$ settling in a stationary cylindrical tube of radius $R+d$, with $d\ll R$ and zero net through-flow, has leading speed
$$
U\sim\frac{8\sqrt2}{27\pi}\frac{\Delta\rho g d^2}{\mu}\sqrt{\frac dR}.
$$
The neck gap is $H(z)=d+z^2/(2R)$. In the <sphere> frame, leading bypass flux per circumferential length is $q\simeq UR/2$; wall-motion transport is smaller by $d/R$. The <pressure-driven flux through a parabolic lubrication gap> gives $\Delta p=9\pi\mu UR\sqrt{2R}/(4d^{5/2})$. <Pressure> dominates the <sphere> drag, $F\simeq\pi R^2\Delta p$, and balancing $F$ against $4\pi R^3\Delta\rho g/3$ proves the speed. Tube end conditions allowing appreciable net flow change the bypass balance and can give other drag regimes.

= Eccentricity increases narrow-gap bypass mobility
{parent=Settling speed of a nearly occluding sphere without through-flow}
{title2=$U(e)/U(0)=(2\pi)^{-1}\int_0^{2\pi}(1+(e/d)\cos\phi)^{5/2}d\phi$}

For an off-axis nearly occluding <sphere> with radial offset $e<d$, the local minimum gap is $d(\phi)=d+e\cos\phi$ to leading order. Each circumferential strip has pressure-driven conductance proportional to $d(\phi)^{5/2}$, and the strips act in parallel. Thus its speed at fixed load is multiplied relative to the concentric value by
$$
\frac1{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}(1+\epsilon\cos\phi)^{5/2}d\phi,\qquad\epsilon=e/d.
$$
<Convexity> and the zero average of cosine make this greater than one for nonzero $\epsilon$. It increases strictly with $\epsilon$: pair the positive and negative cosine contributions to the derivative, obtaining factors $c[(1+\epsilon c)^{3/2}-(1-\epsilon c)^{3/2}]>0$ for $c>0$. Consequently an off-axis <sphere> falls faster in this narrow-gap, no-through-flow regime.

= Control-volume drag formula for a sphere in a tube
{parent=Lubrication resistance integrals for a nearly occluding sphere}
{title2=$F=\pi a^2\Delta P+2\pi a\mu(4UI_1-6qI_2)$}

Take a fluid <control volume> spanning a nearly occluding <sphere>. The <pressure> difference contributes $\pi a^2\Delta P$ upwards, while the outer-wall shear in the <sphere> frame contributes $2\pi a\mu\int u_y(h)\,dz$. Since <sphere-frame flux in a tube> gives $u_y(h)=4U/h-6q/h^2$, the drag on the <sphere> is $F=\pi a^2\Delta P+2\pi a\mu(4UI_1-6qI_2)$. Using the wall shear avoids integrating traction over the strongly curved <sphere> directly. The remaining tube <pressure> drop is fixed by <Hagen-Poiseuille flow>.

= Three drag regimes for a nearly occluding sphere
{parent=Control-volume drag formula for a sphere in a tube}
{title2=$F^*\sim\frac{3\pi L^*\epsilon^{-5/2}+4\pi^2\epsilon^{-3}}{L^*+3\pi\epsilon^{-5/2}}$}

Use $F^*=F/(6\pi\mu aU)$ and $L^*=4L/(3a)$. The <control-volume drag formula for a sphere in a tube> gives the displayed uniform leading drag. If $L^*\ll\epsilon^{-1/2}$, $F^*\sim(4\pi/3)\epsilon^{-1/2}$ and local annular shear dominates. If $\epsilon^{-1/2}\ll L^*\ll\epsilon^{-5/2}$, $F^*\sim L^*$ and the <Hagen-Poiseuille flow> resistance of the long tube dominates. If $L^*\gg\epsilon^{-5/2}$, $F^*\sim3\pi\epsilon^{-5/2}$ and pressure-driven bypass through the narrow gap dominates. The laboratory flow is piston-like in the first two limits and negligible in the third.

= Load sharing between co-moving spheres in a tube
{parent=Three drag regimes for a nearly occluding sphere}
{title2=$F_{\mathrm{each}}\simeq F_{\mathrm{single}}/2\quad\text{in the tube-resistance regime}$}

Two identical well-separated <spheres> falling at the same speed share one laboratory <volume flux>, rather than producing twice the single-sphere piston flux. In the middle of the <three drag regimes for a nearly occluding sphere>, the same overall <Hagen-Poiseuille flow> <pressure> drop is divided equally between them, halving each <sphere>\'s drag. In the local-shear and local-gap-pressure regimes each <sphere> retains its own leading resistance, so the drag per <sphere> is unchanged. This conclusion assumes nonoverlapping lubrication regions and sufficient separation from the tube ends.

= Couette-Poiseuille flow in a thin gap
{parent=Lubrication theory}
{c}

Between surfaces of velocities $U_0,U_1$ separated by $h$, the local lubrication profile is
$$u=U_0+(U_1-U_0)y/h+(p_x/2\mu)y(y-h).$$

= Couette-Poiseuille flow with a stress-free stationary wall
{parent=Couette-Poiseuille flow in a thin gap}
{c}

For $U_0=0$, $U_1=U$, and constant $G=p_x$, zero shear at $y=0$ requires
$$
G=\frac{2\mu U}{h^2}.
$$
The velocity becomes $u=Uy^2/h^2$, the flux per unit width is $Uh/3$, and the fluid exerts shear $-2\mu U/h$ on the moving upper wall.

= Moving-boundary lubrication flux
{parent=Couette-Poiseuille flow in a thin gap}

The flux of the local Couette-Poiseuille profile is
$$Q=h(U_0+U_1)/2-h^3p_x/(12\mu).$$

= Sphere-frame flux in a tube
{parent=Moving-boundary lubrication flux}
{title2=$q=(\pi a^2U-Q)/(2\pi a)$}

For a <sphere> falling with speed $U$ through a tube of radius $a$, let $Q$ be the downward laboratory <volume flux>. In the <sphere> frame the wall moves upwards and the narrow annulus carries upward flux per unit circumference $q=(\pi a^2U-Q)/(2\pi a)$. <Couette-Poiseuille flow in a thin gap> gives $q=Uh/2-h^3p_z/(12\mu)$. Thus the <pressure> below minus above the <sphere> is $\Delta P=6\mu(2qI_3-UI_2)$, using <lubrication resistance integrals for a nearly occluding sphere>. A nearly piston-like laboratory flux can coexist with a nonzero relative gap flux.

= Drag on a plane beneath a translating cylinder
{parent=Moving-boundary lubrication flux}
{title2=$F_x=2\pi\mu U\sqrt{2a/h_0}$}

For a full Newtonian liquid film in the <lubrication approximation>, a cylinder translating parallel to a stationary plane with minimum gap $h_0\ll a$ exerts tangential force $2\pi\mu U\sqrt{2a/h_0}$ per unit axial length on the plane. The leading normal force vanishes by the odd symmetry of excess <pressure>.

= Reynolds equation
{c}
{parent=Moving-boundary lubrication flux}
{wiki}

= Reynolds lubrication equation
{c}
{synonym}

Incompressible mass conservation in a moving gap gives
$$
\partial_t h+\partial_xQ=0.
$$
Combined with the local Couette-Poiseuille flux, this is the Reynolds lubrication equation.

For a thin gap $h$ with tangential wall velocities $\mathbf v_0,\mathbf v_h$, <lubrication theory> and the <no-slip boundary condition> give the integrated <volume flux>
$$
\mathbf q=-\frac{h^3}{12\mu}\nabla_\parallel p+\frac h2(\mathbf v_0+\mathbf v_h).
$$
<Conservation of mass> gives $h_t+\nabla_\parallel\cdot\mathbf q=0$. This is the Reynolds equation; it determines the <lubrication pressure> from wall motion and gap geometry.

= Horizontal rotation in a nearly touching spherical shell
{parent=Reynolds equation}
{title2=$G\sim(16\pi/5)(\mu\Omega a^4/\Delta)\log(1/\epsilon)$}

For $\epsilon=1-\alpha\ll1$, the near-pole gap is $h=\epsilon\Delta+r^2/(2R)$ with $R\simeq a^2/\Delta$, and horizontal rotation gives local sliding speed $V\simeq a\Omega$. The annulus $a\sqrt\epsilon\ll r\ll a$ has $h\sim\Delta r^2/a^2$. Its shear dissipation is at least $\mu V^2\int dA/h\sim2\pi\mu V^2R\log(1/\epsilon)$, so a uniformly bounded torque of order $\mu\Omega a^4/\Delta$ is impossible. The exact local <Reynolds lubrication equation> solution $p=-(6\mu V/5)x/h^2$ gives the displayed logarithmic torque and a dominant horizontal holding-force magnitude $(12\pi/5)(\mu\Omega a^4/\Delta^2)\log(1/\epsilon)$. The smallest patch and fixed-angle outer region each contribute only a bounded amount; the intermediate annulus supplies the logarithm. A fully flooded film is assumed.

= Weakly compliant sphere-plane squeeze flow
{parent=Reynolds equation}
{title2=$H=H_0+R^2/2+\varepsilon P$}

A sphere approaching a locally compliant elastic coating has a <parabolic lubrication gap> increased by pressure-induced downward surface displacement. With a local compliance $\eta$, the dimensionless compliance is $\varepsilon=\eta\mu a\widehat v/\widehat h^3$. The gap is $H=H_0+R^2/2+\varepsilon P$ and its <Reynolds lubrication equation> is $12H_T=R^{-1}(RH^3P_R)_R$. The rigid term is $P_0=-3\dot H_0/(H_0+R^2/2)^2$. The first elastic correction contains both $\dot H_0^2$ and $\ddot H_0$: elastic gap evolution retains the time derivative of pressure even when fluid inertia is negligible.

= Load-controlled breakdown of weak squeeze-flow compliance
{parent=Weakly compliant sphere-plane squeeze flow}
{title2=$H_0^*\sim\sqrt{\varepsilon A},\quad A=F/(6\pi)$}

For <weakly compliant sphere-plane squeeze flow>, the dimensionless upward <force> is $F=6\pi[-\dot H_0/H_0+\varepsilon(\ddot H_0/H_0^3-12\dot H_0^2/(5H_0^4))+\cdots]$. Under fixed positive load, the leading motion is $\dot H_0=-AH_0$. Its elastic force correction is negative, $-(7/5)\varepsilon A^2/H_0^2$, so weak compliance initially increases the approach speed. Pressure indents the wall and widens the gap, reducing squeeze resistance. The perturbation loses uniform validity when $\varepsilon A/H_0^2$ becomes order one. This is also when the elastic deflection becomes comparable to the nominal gap; it does not by itself prove contact or breakdown of the slender-gap approximation.

= Logarithmic lubrication resistance of a sphere near a wall
{parent=Reynolds equation}

A sphere of radius $a$ at gap $a\epsilon$ translates at $U\mathbf e_x$ and rotates at $\omega\mathbf e_y$ above a stationary wall in a right-handed coordinate system. At leading order its gap is $h=a\epsilon+r^2/(2a)$. In its translating frame the lower and upper tangential velocities are $-U\mathbf e_x$ and $-a\omega\mathbf e_x$. The <Reynolds lubrication equation> gives
$$
p=\frac{6\mu}{5}(U+a\omega)\frac{x}{h^2}.
$$
Integrating the upper-boundary <traction>, including pressure on the sloping surface, and its moment about the sphere centre gives the sphere-on-fluid <force> and <torque>
$$
\begin{pmatrix}F_x\\G_y/a\end{pmatrix}\sim\frac{4\pi\mu a}{5}\log(1/\epsilon)\begin{pmatrix}4&-1\\-1&4\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}U\\a\omega\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The off-diagonal symmetry is required by the <Lorentz reciprocal theorem for Stokes flow>. A <torque-free> sphere therefore has $a\omega=U/4$ and leading drag $3\pi\mu aU\log(1/\epsilon)$. Compare the rigid-wall terms of https://vincent-bertin.github.io/Papers/09_Bertin2022JFM.pdf[Bertin et al., equations (4.4)–(4.5)]; those forces act on the sphere and have the opposite sign.

= Logarithmic integrals in a parabolic lubrication gap
{parent=Logarithmic lubrication resistance of a sphere near a wall}

For a fixed outer radius $b\ll a$ and $h=a\epsilon+r^2/(2a)$,
$$
\int_0^b\frac{r^{2n-1}}{h^n}dr=2^{n-1}a^n\log(1/\epsilon)+O(a^n),\qquad n\geq1.
$$
Indeed, for $a\sqrt\epsilon\ll r\ll b$ the integrand is $(2a)^n/r$; integrating from a scale proportional to $a\sqrt\epsilon$ to $b$ produces half of $\log(1/\epsilon)$. The inner region contributes a bounded term after rescaling $r=a\sqrt\epsilon\,s$. In particular, $\int h^{-1}dxdy$ and $\int x^2/(ah^2)dxdy$ have the same leading coefficient $2\pi a$.

= Pressure gradient in a translating and squeezing finite gap
{parent=Reynolds equation}

For lower speed $U$, stationary upper tangential speed, upper normal speed $-V$, and equal endpoint pressures, define $\langle f\rangle=L^{-1}\int_0^Lf(x)\,dx$. Then
$$
p_x=\frac{6\mu U}{h^2}-\frac{12\mu(Vx+C)}{h^3},
$$
where
$$
C=\frac U2\frac{\langle h^{-2}\rangle}{\langle h^{-3}\rangle}
-V\frac{\langle xh^{-3}\rangle}{\langle h^{-3}\rangle}.
$$

= Zero-shear lower wall in a translating and squeezing finite gap
{parent=Pressure gradient in a translating and squeezing finite gap}

The horizontal force on the lower wall vanishes when
$$
U=6V\frac{
\langle xh^{-2}\rangle
-\langle h^{-2}\rangle\langle xh^{-3}\rangle/
\langle h^{-3}\rangle}
{4\langle h^{-1}\rangle
-3\langle h^{-2}\rangle^2/\langle h^{-3}\rangle}.
$$

= Annular lubrication drag on a settling cylinder
{parent=Moving-boundary lubrication flux}

For a cylinder of radius $a$ in a coaxial container with gap $h\ll a$, global displacement forces reverse flow through the gap and creates
$$
\Delta p\simeq\frac{6\mu aLU}{h^3}.
$$
The pressure drag exceeds side shear by order $a/h$. Balancing it with excess weight gives $U\simeq\Delta\rho,gh^3/(6\mu a)$.

= Pressure recovery condition in lubrication flow
{parent=Lubrication theory}

If pressure approaches the same ambient value at both ends of a lubrication region, then $\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}p_x\,dx=0$, which determines the conserved flux.

= Nearly occluding sphere in a cylindrical tube
{parent=Lubrication theory}

For a sphere of radius $(1-\epsilon)a$ displaced by $\epsilon\lambda a$ from the axis of a tube of radius $a$, the narrow gap is
$$
h(\theta,x)=\epsilon a(1+\lambda\cos\theta)+\frac{x^2}{2a}.
$$
Its thickness is $O(\epsilon a)$ and its axial length is $O(a\sqrt\epsilon)$, so <lubrication pressure dominates shear stress> by $O(\epsilon^{-1/2})$.

= Force-free transport of a nearly occluding sphere
{parent=Nearly occluding sphere in a cylindrical tube}
{title2=$q=-2Uh_0/3,\quad\Delta p\sim(2\mu U/a)\sqrt{2/\epsilon}\int_0^{2\pi}(1+\lambda\cos\theta)^{-1/2}d\theta$}

For a nonrotating <force-free> <sphere> moving with speed $U$ in a stationary tube, use its moving frame: the tube wall has velocity $-U$. In the <parabolic lubrication gap> $h=h_0+x^2/(2a)$, $h_0=\epsilon a(1+\lambda\cos\theta)$, <moving-boundary lubrication flux> gives $q=-h^3p_x/(12\mu)-Uh/2$. The <wall-shear and pressure-drop balance in a tube> rules out an $O(\epsilon^{-3/2})$ net pressure jump. Applying this common pressure recovery to each angular strip gives $q=-2Uh_0/3$. The next balance yields the displayed positive localized pressure drop. Meanwhile the integrated leading <shear stress> on the sphere vanishes in each strip; this is consistent with negligible leading torque, and does not mean the local shear or pressure force vanishes.

= Wall-shear and pressure-drop balance in a tube
{parent=Nearly occluding sphere in a cylindrical tube}

An axial force balance on fluid between two remote cross-sections of a cylindrical tube gives
$$
a\int_{x_1}^{x_2}\int_0^{2\pi}\sigma_{rx}(a,\theta,x)\,d\theta\,dx
=\pi a^2[p(x_2)-p(x_1)]
$$
when every immersed body between the sections is <force-free>.

= Thin-film equation
{parent=Lubrication theory}
{wiki}

A thin-film equation combines a lubrication flux with mass conservation to evolve a free-surface thickness. Gravity, capillary pressure, wall motion, and intermolecular forces may contribute to the flux.

= Thermocapillary thin-film equation
{parent=Thin-film equation}
{title2=$h_t+\partial_x(a_ch^3h_{xxx}+a_mh^2h_x)=0$}

= Thermocapillary film instability
{synonym}

For a liquid film heated below and cooled above, the <quasistatic temperature of a conductively cooled film> makes a thicker region colder at the surface. If <surface tension> decreases with temperature, <Marangoni stress> draws fluid towards thicker regions, competing with smoothing by <capillary pressure>. Writing $\gamma_s=\gamma_0+bh$ gives $a_c=\gamma_0/(3\mu)$, $a_m=b/(2\mu)$. A balanced long-wave scaling gives $H_\tau+(H^2H_X)_X+(H^3H_{XXX})_X=0$. The <dispersion relation> $s=k^2-k^4$ has unstable band $0<|k|<1$ and fastest <normal mode> at $|k|=1/\sqrt2$. This thermal mechanism does not require <surfactant>.

= Zero-flux thermocapillary film profiles
{parent=Thermocapillary thin-film equation}
{title2=$H_X^2/2+H(\ln H-1)=E$}

Positive steady solutions of the balanced <thermocapillary thin-film equation> with zero <volume flux> satisfy $H_{XX}+\ln H=0$ after choosing the thickness scale. Their first integral has potential $V(H)=H(\ln H-1)$, with minimum $V(1)=-1$ and limiting value $V(0^+)=0$. At $E=-1$ the film is uniform; for $-1<E<0$ it is positive and periodic. At $E=0$ a limiting drop has peak $e$, half-width $\sqrt{\pi e}$, zero limiting <contact angle> and divergent curvature at zero thickness. Small periodic profiles have <period> $2\pi$ and approach the neutral, rather than fastest-growing, <normal mode> of the <thermocapillary film instability>.

= Quasistatic temperature of a conductively cooled film
{parent=Thermocapillary thin-film equation}
{title2=$T_s=T_0-\Delta T\alpha h/(\kappa+\alpha h)$}

For a film with fixed lower temperature $T_0$ and upper condition $-\kappa T_z=\alpha(T_s-T_0+\Delta T)$, negligible horizontal thermal <diffusion> and thermal <advection> reduce the <heat equation> to $T_{zz}=0$. Thus $T=T_0-\alpha\Delta T z/(\kappa+\alpha h)$. The local approximation requires $(h/L)^2\ll1$ and $Uh^2/(\kappa L)\ll1$, together with slow time variation relative to $h^2/\kappa$. For $\alpha h/\kappa\ll1$, $T_s\simeq T_0-\Delta T\alpha h/\kappa$. The coefficient $\kappa$ is <thermal diffusivity>, and the boundary coefficient $\alpha$ is normalized accordingly.

= Thin liquid film on a vertical cylinder
{parent=Thin-film equation}

For an exterior thin film, circumferential and axial curvature together give $h_t+(h^3[h_{zzz}+h_z+G])_z/3=0$ in appropriate dimensionless variables. The $h_z$ term destabilizes long waves through the <Rayleigh–Plateau instability>, whereas $h_{zzz}$ damps short waves. The linear growth rate is $(k^2-k^4)/3-iGk$, with fastest growth at $|k|=1/\sqrt2$. The gravity parameter is $G=\rho ga^2/(\gamma\epsilon)$ when film thickness is scaled as $\epsilon a h$ and axial distance by cylinder radius $a$.

= Large solitary pulse on a cylindrical film
{parent=Thin liquid film on a vertical cylinder}
{title2=$H_{\max}\sim2\kappa(3c)^{2/3}$}

For a <travelling wave> of large positive speed in the cylindrical film equation, the leading core is proportional to $1-\cos x$ over an axial interval of length $2\pi$. Edge coordinates $X=(3c)^{1/3}(x-x_e)$ reduce the transition equation to $H^3H_{XXX}=H-1$. Its real positive exponent gives a monotone trailing approach and its complex stable exponents give damped <capillary waves> ahead, with physical wavelength $4\pi a/(3^{5/6}c^{1/3})$. Matching the two quadratic edge expansions selects the uniform film thickness; this is a <matched asymptotic expansion>, valid while the pulse itself remains thin relative to the cylinder.

= Thin-film equations with insoluble surfactant
{parent=Thin-film equation}
{title2=$h_t+q_x=0,\quad C_t+(Cu_s)_x=0$}

For a no-slip planar liquid film with an <insoluble surfactant>, <lubrication theory> gives $q=-h^3p_x/(3\mu)+h^2\gamma_x/(2\mu)$ and surface <velocity> $u_s=-h^2p_x/(2\mu)+h\gamma_x/\mu$. <Mass conservation> and <conservation of insoluble surfactant on a moving interface> give $h_t+q_x=0$ and $C_t+(Cu_s)_x=0$. With $\gamma=\gamma_0-AC$ and small slopes, $p_x=\rho gh_x-(\gamma h_{xx})_x$. The different coefficients in $q$ and $u_s$ are essential: <Marangoni stress> and hydrostatic return flow transport fluid and <surfactant> differently.

= Zero-liquid-flux surfactant film
{parent=Thin-film equations with insoluble surfactant}
{title2=$Q=0$}

In the same gravity–Marangoni reduction, zero liquid flux gives $\Gamma=1+(H_0^2-H^2)/3$ and $6JX=(1+H_0^2/3)(H^3-H_0^3)/3-(H^5-H_0^5)/15$. The lower part of the liquid flows backwards while the surface and upper third flow forwards. For zero initial height and concentration drop $\delta$, $J=\delta^{3/2}(1-3\delta/5)/(2\sqrt3)$ is largest formally at complete depletion. Since surface <velocity> is $J/\Gamma$, that endpoint is singular and requires physical regularization by diffusion, capillarity or an edge model. The algebraic maximum is not by itself a regular zero-concentration outflow.

= Steady surfactant film with zero surface flux
{parent=Thin-film equations with insoluble surfactant}
{title2=$J=0$}

In the steady nondiffusive gravity–Marangoni model with capillarity neglected, zero surfactant flux and positive concentration make the surface <velocity> zero. With $Q=-H^2\Gamma_X/2-H^3H_X/3$ and $J=-H\Gamma\Gamma_X-H^2\Gamma H_X/2$, integration gives $H^4=H_0^4-48QX$ and $\Gamma=1+(H_0^2-H^2)/4$. A concentration drop $\delta$ over unit length therefore selects $Q=-\delta(H_0^2+2\delta)/6$. The liquid flows opposite to the imposed Marangoni traction, with <velocity> proportional to $H_XY(Y-H)/2$ and an immobilized surface. This reduction is singular at a dry endpoint; its small-slope assumption does not resolve that edge.

= Finite-mass Marangoni spreading on a liquid film
{parent=Thin-film equations with insoluble surfactant}
{title2=$\ell\sim(AMh_0t/\mu)^{1/3}$}

A localized amount $M=\int C\,dx$ of <insoluble surfactant> spreads over a planar film of depth scale $h_0$. Neglect gravity, capillarity and diffusion. Then $C\sim M/\ell$ and <Marangoni stress> drives $u_s\sim AMh_0/(\mu\ell^2)$, giving $\ell\sim(AMh_0t/\mu)^{1/3}$. The <thin-film equations with insoluble surfactant> admit a <similarity solution> in which the film height and concentration depend on $x/\ell$, conserving total <surfactant> and fluid volume relative to the undisturbed layer.

= Gravity smoothing of a Marangoni front
{parent=Finite-mass Marangoni spreading on a liquid film}
{title2=$\Delta\sim\rho gh_0^2x_N^2/(AM)$}

When the smoothing width exceeds the <capillary length>, gravity dominates capillarity. Balancing hydrostatic flux $\rho gh_0^4/(\mu\Delta)$ against the <Marangoni stress> flux gives $\Delta\sim\rho gh_0^2x_N^2/(AM)\propto t^{2/3}$. The condition is $\Delta^2\gg\gamma_0/(\rho g)$ in physical units. The smoothing region catches up with the spreading pool at $x_N\sim AM/(\rho gh_0^2)$ and $t\sim\mu(AM)^2/[(\rho g)^3h_0^7]$, after which the sharp-front outer <similarity solution> ceases to apply.

= Gravity-levelled surfactant film
{parent=Gravity smoothing of a Marangoni front}
{title2=$h_x\simeq-3AC_x/(2\rho gh_0)$}

After <gravity smoothing of a Marangoni front> reaches the whole spreading pool, the film approaches uniform depth while surface transport continues. A small height gradient creates a <hydrostatic pressure> return flow that almost cancels the net <Marangoni stress> flux. Setting $q\simeq0$ in the <thin-film equations with insoluble surfactant> gives $h_x\simeq-3AC_x/(2\rho gh_0)$ and fractional depth variation $O(AM/(\rho gh_0^2x_N))\ll1$. The depressed region under the <surfactant> therefore becomes progressively shallower.

= Capillary smoothing of a Marangoni front
{parent=Finite-mass Marangoni spreading on a liquid film}
{title2=$\Delta\sim(\gamma_0h_0^2x_N^2/(AM))^{1/3}$}

The height jump in <linear similarity profiles for surfactant spreading> is smoothed by <surface tension>. If $C_x\sim M/x_N^2$ through a region of width $\Delta$, the <Marangoni stress> flux is $AMh_0^2/(\mu x_N^2)$ and the capillary flux is $\gamma_0h_0^4/(\mu\Delta^3)$. Their balance gives $\Delta\sim(\gamma_0h_0^2x_N^2/(AM))^{1/3}\propto t^{2/9}$. This estimate assumes gravity is negligible and the smoothing region remains narrow compared with the pool.

= Linear similarity profiles for surfactant spreading
{parent=Finite-mass Marangoni spreading on a liquid film}
{title2=$x_N=(6AMh_0t/\mu)^{1/3}$}

For symmetric <finite-mass Marangoni spreading on a liquid film>, the leading outer profiles are $h=2h_0|x|/x_N$ and $C=(M/x_N)(1-|x|/x_N)$ inside $|x|<x_N$, where $x_N=(6AMh_0t/\mu)^{1/3}$. Outside, $h=h_0$ and $C=0$. The <similarity solution> follows from $H\Gamma^{\prime}=-\eta/3$, $\eta H^{\prime}=H$, $2\int\Gamma\,d\eta=1$ and $\int(H-1)\,d\eta=0$. The film reaches height $2h_0$ immediately behind the front, leaving an outer height jump that gravity or <surface tension> must smooth. These are ideal leading outer profiles, not a description of the microscopic central dry region or the smoothed edge.

= Disjoining pressure
{title2=$\Pi(h)$}
{parent=Thin-film equation}
{wiki}

Disjoining pressure is the thickness-dependent normal stress caused by intermolecular interactions between nearby interfaces. An attractive law $\Pi(h)=V/h^3$ grows as a film thins and can drive rupture.

= Van der Waals rupture instability of a viscous sheet
{c}
{parent=Disjoining pressure}

For a viscous sheet of half-thickness $h_0$, attractive <disjoining pressure> $V/h^3$, and <surface tension> $\gamma$, a symmetric Fourier mode of dimensionless wavenumber $K=kh_0$ has a destabilizing molecular contribution and a stabilizing capillary contribution. The ratio
$$
\Gamma=\frac{\gamma h_0^2}{3V}
$$
sets the cutoff $K=\Gamma^{-1/2}$.

= Clean-sheet long-wave rupture plateau
{parent=Van der Waals rupture instability of a viscous sheet}
{title2=$\lim_{k\to0,\ k\ne0}s=3V/(4\mu h_0^3)$}

A sheet with stress-free surfaces supports nearly uniform cross-thickness extensional motion. Its diverging long-wave mobility compensates the weak pressure gradient, leaving the nonzero rate limit in the creeping-flow model. The exactly uniform thickness change is excluded at fixed volume, and unsteady inertia or finite domain size can regularize the limiting prediction. Strong surfactant immobilization instead makes the rate vanish quadratically with wave number.

= Thin-film mass flux
{parent=Thin-film equation}

The thin-film mass flux is the layer thickness times its depth-averaged velocity. In a quasi-steady transition region, mass conservation makes this flux approximately constant along the layer.

= Slender viscous bubble
{parent=Lubrication theory}

For a long axisymmetric bubble with slowly varying radius $a(z,t)$, the leading normal-stress balance and internal lubrication flux couple capillary pressure to conservation of the cross-sectional area.

= Lubrication equation for a bubble with viscous exterior
{parent=Slender viscous bubble}
{title2=$(a^2)_t=(8\lambda\mu)^{-1}[a^4(2\mu a_t/a+\gamma/a)_z]_z$}

Internal <Hagen-Poiseuille flow> of viscosity $\lambda\mu$ has axial volume flux $Q=-\pi a^4P_z/(8\lambda\mu)$ when the interfacial axial speed is negligible to leading order. <Conservation of mass> gives $(\pi a^2)_t+Q_z=0$. Combining with the exterior radial normal-stress balance gives the displayed implicit evolution equation. The radial viscous normal stress and capillary pressure both matter in its local pinch-off similarity.

= Uniform-pressure evolution of a slender viscous bubble
{parent=Slender viscous bubble}
{title2=$P=\gamma f/\alpha_0,\quad\dot f=-\gamma g^2/(2\mu\alpha_0),\quad\dot g=\gamma fg/(2\mu\alpha_0)$}

A periodic slender bubble with $a=f+\sqrt2g\sin kz$ and negligible internal pressure variation has exterior radial velocity $u_r=a\dot a/r$ and pressure $P=2\mu\dot a/a+\gamma/a$. Its conserved mean cross-sectional area gives $\alpha_0=f^2+g^2=f(0)^2+g(0)^2$. Comparing the constant and sinusoidal terms gives the displayed equations. The long-wave growth rate about radius $a_0$ is $\gamma/(2\mu a_0)$, and $\dot a_{\min}=-\gamma(g^2+\sqrt2fg)/(2\mu\alpha_0)<0$. Small internal viscosity eventually produces appreciable axial pressure gradients near a neck, so this uniform-pressure reduction cannot persist to pinch-off at fixed positive viscosity ratio.

= Viscous-capillary pinch-off similarity
{parent=Slender viscous bubble}

Near a finite-time viscous-capillary pinch-off, balancing time dependence, viscous normal stress, capillary pressure, and axial lubrication flow can select self-similar radius and axial scales.

= Linear pinch-off scaling of a slender viscous bubble
{parent=Viscous-capillary pinch-off similarity}
{title2=$a=(\gamma/\mu)\tau A(\zeta),\quad\zeta=\mu\sqrt\lambda(z-z_*)/(\gamma\tau)$}

For $\tau=t_*-t$, balance $\mu a_t/a\sim\gamma/a$ to obtain radius proportional to $\tau$. Axial lubrication conservation then makes the axial length proportional to $\tau$ as well. The displayed variables reduce the <lubrication equation for a bubble with viscous exterior> to $\zeta AA'-A^2=\tfrac1{16}\{A^4[(2\zeta A'+1)/A]'\}'$. The physical interface slope is $\sqrt\lambda A'$, compatible with a slender neck when $\lambda\ll1$ and the dimensionless profile varies on order-one scales.

= Slender viscous thread
{parent=Lubrication theory}

A slender viscous thread has a cross-sectional scale much smaller than its axial variation scale. For an axisymmetric Newtonian thread of area $A$ and nearly uniform axial velocity $w$, <mass conservation> and the <Trouton ratio> give
$$
A_t+(Aw)_z=0,
\qquad
\rho gA+\partial_z(3\mu A w_z)=0
$$
when inertia and <surface tension> are negligible and $z$ points downward.

= Annular viscous extension
{parent=Slender viscous thread}

Uniform <axial strain rate> $E$ in an annular <Newtonian fluid> gives radial <velocity> $u_r=-Er/2+C/r$. For radii $R_i,R_e$, area parameter $A=R_e^2-R_i^2$, and constant gas <pressures>,
$$
C=\frac{R_i^2R_e^2}{2\mu A}\left[p_i-p_e-\gamma\left(\frac1{R_i}+\frac1{R_e}\right)\right],\qquad
p=p_e-\frac{(p_i-p_e)R_i^2}{A}+\frac{\gamma(R_e+R_i)}A-\mu E.
$$
The opposite signs of inner and outer curvature in the <Young–Laplace equation> give these expressions. The <kinematic boundary condition> implies $\dot A=-EA$ and
$$
\frac d{dt}(R_e-R_i)=-\frac E2(R_e-R_i)+\frac\gamma{2\mu}-\frac{(p_i-p_e)R_iR_e}{2\mu(R_i+R_e)}.
$$

= Capillary collapse of an annular viscous cylinder
{parent=Annular viscous extension}

For equal internal and external <pressures>, zero <axial strain rate> and positive <surface tension>, <annular viscous extension> gives constant $A=R_e^2-R_i^2$ and thickness $D(t)=R_e(0)-R_i(0)+\gamma t/(2\mu)$. Since $R_i=(A/D-D)/2$, the central hole closes at
$$
t_* =\frac{2\mu}{\gamma}\left[\sqrt{A(0)}-R_e(0)+R_i(0)\right].
$$
The formula holds until closure and requires a genuine initial annulus; the subsequent topology has no inner cylindrical interface.

= Capillary tensile force of a slender thread
{parent=Slender viscous thread}
{title2=$T=3\pi\mu a^2w_z+\pi\gamma a$}

For an axisymmetric <slender viscous thread>, the <Young–Laplace equation> and radial extensional <viscous stress> give $p=p_e+\gamma/a-\mu w_z$. The axial liquid stress relative to ambient <pressure> is $3\mu w_z-\gamma/a$. Its cross-sectional <integral> plus the circumferential <surface tension> <force> $2\pi\gamma a$ gives the displayed tensile <force>. Thus the net capillary contribution is $\pi\gamma a$, and axial <force> balance with downward gravity is $T_z+\pi\rho ga^2=0$.

= Capillary tensile force of a hollow slender thread
{parent=Capillary tensile force of a slender thread}
{title2=$T/\pi=3\mu Aw_z+\gamma(R_e+R_i)$}

For a hollow <slender viscous thread>, $A=R_e^2-R_i^2$ and both surfaces contribute axial <surface tension>. The liquid cut <force> relative to exterior <pressure>, including these surface terms, is $\pi[3\mu Aw_z+(p_i-p_e)R_i^2+\gamma(R_e+R_i)]$. The axial traction on the sloping inner gas surface contributes $-\pi(p_i-p_e)(R_i^2)_z$ to <force> balance. For constant gas <pressures> the extra derivative cancels, giving $3\mu(Aw_z)_z+\rho gA+\gamma(R_e+R_i)_z=0$. The displayed $T$ is this effective tension after accounting for gas-pressure work, not the unadjusted liquid cut <force>.

= Capillary closure of a falling hollow thread
{parent=Capillary tensile force of a hollow slender thread}

For steady equal-pressure flow, <mass conservation> gives $Aw=A_0w_0$ and the <kinematic boundary condition> gives $(D\sqrt w)_z=\gamma/(2\mu\sqrt w)$, where $D=R_e-R_i$. Therefore finite-height closure occurs exactly when
$$
\frac\gamma{2\mu\sqrt{w_0}}\int_0^{z_*}\frac{dz}{\sqrt{w(z)}}=\sqrt{A_0}-D_0.
$$
For positive <velocity> the <integral> is increasing. If it diverges, closure is finite; if its limiting value lies below the right-hand threshold, there is no finite closure. Quadratic freely falling profiles have logarithmically divergent <integrals>, while sufficiently strongly drawn exponentially accelerating profiles can have finite <integrals>. Thus nozzle speed alone is insufficient to guarantee closure: the axial tension or downstream condition matters.

= Viscous-thread drawing
{parent=Slender viscous thread}

In steady viscous-thread drawing the conserved volume flux $Aw=Q$ couples thinning directly to acceleration. The axial tensile stress is $3\mu w_z$, so solutions with larger positive $w_z$ thin more rapidly.

= Localized-neck and uniform-thread stretching exponents
{parent=Viscous-thread drawing}
{title2=$L\propto(t^*-t)^{-1/2}\text{ or }(t^*-t)^{-1}$}

A constant-tension <slender viscous thread> loses the same cross-sectional area along each material element: $A=A_0-Ft/(3\mu)$. Volume conservation gives material stretch $A_0/A$. Near an isolated quadratic minimum of $A_0$, integration over a shrinking material width proportional to $(t^*-t)^{1/2}$ gives total extension exponent $-1/2$. A uniformly thin thread has the same divergent stretch everywhere and exponent $-1$. The localized coefficient is independent of remote endpoints.

= Viscous-sheet drawing
{parent=Slender viscous thread}

Viscous-sheet drawing stretches a thin ribbon between rollers. <Mass conservation>, axial force balance, lateral <surface tension>, and edge kinematics jointly determine its half-thickness, half-width, and axial speed.

= Viscous shear torque
{parent=Viscous fluid flow}

Tangential viscous traction produces torque equal to its moment integrated over the solid surface.

= Torque-free cylinder in a lubrication gap
{parent=Viscous shear torque}

For a circular cylinder translating parallel to a nearby wall at zero Reynolds number, leading lubrication shear and pressure recovery make the zero-torque condition force zero angular velocity.

= Falling cylinder near a wall
{parent=Torque-free cylinder in a lubrication gap}

For a cylinder falling parallel to a wall through a minimum gap $\epsilon a/2$, the <parabolic lubrication gap> has length scale $a\sqrt\epsilon$. The leading tangential drag scales as $\mu U/\sqrt\epsilon$, while the leading rotational resistance scales as $\mu\Omega a/\sqrt\epsilon$.

= Shear and pressure force partition in cylinder lubrication
{parent=Falling cylinder near a wall}
{title2=$F_w^{s}=2\mu UJ_1,\quad F_c^{s}=-2\mu WJ_1,\quad F_c^{p}=2\mu(W-U)J_1$}

In the translating-cylinder frame, a wall moves at $-U$ and the nearby cylinder surface at $W$. With gap $d+x^2/(2a)$ and equal outer pressures, <lubrication theory> gives the displayed forces with $J_1=\pi\sqrt{2a/d}$. Wall and cylinder shear forces alone do not balance because the sloping cylinder's pressure force is of the same order. Their complete sum is zero. Pressure exerts no moment about the circular cylinder center, while shear supplies the leading singular rotational resistance.

= Force-free cylinder in confined Couette flow
{parent=Torque-free cylinder in a lubrication gap}

A nearly confined cylinder in <Couette flow> is driven by the unequal lubrication resistances of its two gaps. If their minimum widths are proportional to $1+\lambda$ and $1-\lambda$, force balance weights the relative wall velocities by the inverse square roots of those widths.
